nchanged?",answer:"Because the matching character does not exist. Unicode has no small capital Q or X, no superscript q, and no convincing upside down Q, so those letters pass through as they are. It is a gap in the standard rather than a bug in the tool, and there is no substitute that does not mean something else entirely."},{question:"Will everyone see the styled text?",answer:"Almost everyone, but not quite. Rendering depends on the fonts installed on the reader device, and older Android builds in particular ship thin coverage of the newer blocks. When a device has no glyph it draws an empty rectangle. Bold, italic and script are the safest; squared, circled and inverted styles are the ones most likely to break."},{question:"Does styled text hurt reach or accessibility?",answer:'Accessibility, yes, and that is the honest answer. A screen reader announces these characters one at a time, as "mathematical bold capital H" or similar, or skips them silently. A whole caption in styled text is unusable for a blind reader. Reach is less clear cut, but in-app search matches literal characters, so a styled word will not be found by someone typing the plain one.'},{question:"Can I use these fonts in my bio?",answer:"Yes, and the bio is where they work best. Every network here accepts pasted Unicode in the bio field, and a bio is short enough that one styled line stays readable. Paste it into the app or the website, do not retype it, because typing loses the special characters."},{question:"Which networks keep styled text and which strip it?",answer:"Instagram, Facebook, X, TikTok, LinkedIn and Threads all keep it in bios, captions and posts, because they store your text as written. What varies is where it is worth using: LinkedIn will render it but the audience reads it as spam, and any network that indexes text for search treats a styled word as a different word."}],relatedToolSlugs:["instagram-fonts-generator","linkedin-fonts-generator","instagram-caption-spacer","social