eadline and the About section all keep the characters as pasted. Where it works against you is anywhere LinkedIn treats text as data: member search, recruiter search, skills and job title matching all read literal characters, so styled text in those fields is text the platform cannot match."}],relatedToolSlugs:["linkedin-character-counter","social-media-fonts-generator","twitter-fonts-generator","facebook-fonts-generator"]},"instagram-caption-spacer":{slug:"instagram-caption-spacer",family:"caption-spacer",platform:"instagram",datePublished:w,lastUpdated:w,label:"Instagram caption spacer",metaTitle:"Instagram Caption Spacer and Line Break Generator",metaDescription:"Free Instagram caption spacer. Paste a caption with line breaks and get one back that keeps them, using an invisible character Instagram will not strip.",heroEyebrow:"Free Instagram tool",heroTitle:"Instagram Caption Spacer",heroLead:"Instagram strips the blank lines out of a caption the moment you publish. Paste yours here and get it back with an invisible character holding each gap open.",intro:["You write a caption with paragraphs, you check it looks right, you publish, and it comes out as one solid block. This is the single most reported piece of weirdness in the Instagram composer, and it is not a bug you can work around by pressing return harder.","Instagram does two things to your text on the way in. It removes trailing spaces and tabs from the end of every line, and then it discards any line that is now empty. A line you left blank for spacing is empty by definition, so it disappears and the paragraphs on either side of it join up.","The fix is to make the blank line not blank. This tool trims the trailing whitespace itself, then drops a single invisible character onto every empty line, so Instagram sees a line with content on it and keeps it. Your spacing survives. Nothing is uploaded and nothing is stored."],tips:["Draft the caption somewhere with a real text field, paste it here, and copy the 