ank lines in a post as written, so a spacer just adds characters for nothing. Instagram is the outlier that strips them, which is why this tool is built around it."},{question:"Is my caption stored or sent anywhere?",answer:"No. The spacing happens in your browser with JavaScript, nothing is sent to a server, and nothing is saved. There is no account step and no email gate. Close the tab and the text is gone."},{question:"Can I use the spacer in my Instagram bio?",answer:"Yes, and the bio needs it more than captions do. The bio field collapses line breaks in much the same way, so pasting a spaced version is the usual way people get a bio to sit on several lines. Remember the bio is only 150 characters, and each separator spends two of them."}],relatedToolSlugs:["instagram-character-counter","instagram-fonts-generator","social-media-character-counter","threads-character-counter"]},"social-media-image-resizer":{slug:"social-media-image-resizer",family:"image-resizer",platform:null,datePublished:U,lastUpdated:U,label:"Social media image resizer",metaTitle:"Social Media Image Resizer for 8 Networks",metaDescription:"Free image resizer: crop one photo to Instagram 1080 x 1350, Story 1080 x 1920, Pinterest 1000 x 1500 and more. Runs in your browser, nothing is uploaded.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"Social Media Image Resizer",heroLead:"Drop one photo in and crop it to every size the 8 networks ask for, from the 1080 x 1350 Instagram feed post to the 1280 x 720 YouTube thumbnail, without uploading anything.",intro:["One photo, thirteen different frames. Instagram wants 1080 x 1350 in the feed and 1080 x 1920 in a Story. Pinterest builds its whole feed around 1000 x 1500. LinkedIn is happiest at 1200 x 627, X crops the timeline to 16:9, and a YouTube thumbnail has to be 1280 x 720 and under 2 MB. Almost nothing you shoot fits more than two of those without a crop.","This resizer does the cropping properly rather than squashing. It fills each target frame completely, keeps the original proportions, and lets you say which part of the photo matters: pick one of nine focus points and every selected size re-crops around it, so a face in the top third stays in frame when the same photo becomes a 9:16 Story and a 16:9 thumbnail.","It all runs on your device. The file is decoded and drawn with canvas inside the tab you are reading, nothing is sent to a server, and no copy is kept. Pick the sizes you want, hit download on each, and close the tab."],tips:["Pick the aspect ratio before the resolution. A 4:5 portrait takes about 25 percent more vertical space in a feed than the same photo at 1:1, and that extra height is free reach on the exact same picture.","Export photos as JPEG and flat graphics as PNG. A photograph at JPEG quality 0.85 is usually a third of the size of the PNG with no visible difference, but a screenshot or a logo with hard edges shows JPEG artifacts around every line.","Do not chase resolution above the recommended size. Instagram re-compresses every upload to 1080 pixels wide and YouTube re-encodes everything, so a 4K export is a slower upload that arrives at the same place.","Keep text and faces out of the outer tenth of the frame on anything vertical. Stories and full screen surfaces put a profile row across the top and a reply bar across the bottom, and both sit over your image rather than beside it.","Resize last. Crop, color and retouch on the full resolution original, then export the small versions from it. Every re-encode of an already compressed JPEG costs a little more detail.","Never upscale to hit a number. A 640 pixel wide source stretched to 1080 looks soft in the feed, and it will look worse after the platform compresses it again. Shoot or export larger instead."],faqs:[{question:"Is my image uploaded anywhere?",answer:"No. The file is read, decoded and redrawn with canvas inside your browser, and the resized copy is handed straight to your downloads folder. Nothing is sent to us or to anyone else, there is no account step, and closing the tab is all the cleanup there is."},{question:"Why will my iPhone photo not open?",answer:"It is almost certainly a HEIC file. That is the format iPhones save by default, and outside Safari most browsers cannot decode it at all, so the tool has nothing to draw. In Photos, use Share and pick a JPEG copy, or change Settings, Camera, Formats to Most Compatible so new photos are saved as JPEG."},{question:"Does resizing remove the location data from my photo?",answer:"Yes. Resizing here means drawing the pixels into a fresh canvas and encoding a new file, and that new file carries no EXIF block at all. GPS coordinates, camera model, serial number, date and time are all left behind. That is a genuine privacy win, and it also means a resized copy is not the one to keep for your archive."},{question:"Will resizing lower the quality of my image?",answer:"Scaling down is the safe direction and usually looks fine. What costs quality is the JPEG encode, which is why there is a quality slider: 0.85 is a good default, 0.9 to 1.0 is worth it for images with text or fine detail, and below 0.75 you start to see blocking in flat areas like sky. PNG is lossless, so the slider does not apply to it."},{question:"What size should a social media image be?",answer:"There is no single answer, which is the reason this tool exists. Instagram feed and Threads want 1080 x 1350, Instagram and Facebook Stories want 1080 x 1920, Facebook feed wants 1200 x 630, LinkedIn 1200 x 627, X 1600 x 900, TikTok photo posts 1080 x 1920, Pinterest 1000 x 1500, and a YouTube thumbnail 1280 x 720. The full table with file size caps is on this page."},{question:"Should I export as JPEG or PNG?",answer:"JPEG for anything photographic, PNG for anything with flat color, sharp text or a logo. PNG files are often five to ten times larger, which matters against a 4 MB Facebook cap or a 2 MB YouTube thumbnail cap. Note that TikTok photo posts accept JPEG and WEBP but not PNG."}],relatedToolSlugs:["instagram-image-resizer","youtube-image-resizer","pinterest-image-resizer","social-media-video-size-checker"]},"instagram-image-resizer":{slug:"instagram-image-resizer",family:"image-resizer",platform:"instagram",datePublished:U,lastUpdated:U,label:"Instagram image resizer",metaTitle:"Instagram Image Resizer: 1080 x 1350 and 1080 x 1920",metaDescription:"Resize an image for Instagram: crop to 1080 x 1350 for the feed, 1080 x 1920 for a Story, or matching carousel slides. Free, in your browser, no upload.",heroEyebrow:"Free Instagram tool",heroTitle:"Instagram Image Resizer",heroLead:"Crop one photo to the three sizes Instagram actually uses: 1080 x 1350 for a feed post, 1080 x 1920 for a Story, and 1080 x 1350 again for carousel slides that all match.",intro:["Instagram publishes one recommended size per surface and quietly crops anything else. A feed image should be 1080 x 1350 at 4:5, a Story should be 1080 x 1920 at 9:16, and every slide in a carousel is forced into the aspect ratio of the first slide, which is why one landscape photo in a portrait set ruins the whole post.","The 4:5 recommendation is not arbitrary. Portrait is the tallest shape the feed will render, so the same photograph occupies noticeably more of a phone screen at 1080 x 1350 than it does at 1080 x 1080. That is the cheapest reach improvement available on the platform, and it costs one crop.","This resizer produces all three at once from a single source. Choose the focus point, and the subject stays in frame whether the crop is going tall for a Story or square-ish for the feed. Files are capped at 8 MB for a feed image, and Instagram re-compresses everything to 1080 pixels wide on the way in, so there is nothing to gain from exporting larger."],tips:["Use 4:5 in the feed, not 1:1. At 1080 x 1350 a post fills more of the screen than the same image at 1080 x 1080, and Instagram treats both as native.","Export at exactly 1080 pixels wide. Instagram re-compresses every upload down to that width anyway, so a 4000 pixel export only makes the upload slower and adds a second round of compression.","Keep a feed image under the 8 MB cap. JPEG at quality 0.85 puts a 1080 x 1350 photo comfortably under 1 MB, so the cap is only ever a problem for PNG exports of detailed images.","Crop every carousel slide to the same shape before you upload. Instagram forces the whole set into the aspect ratio of slide one, so a single 16:9 photo in a 4:5 carousel gets cut in half.","On Stories, keep text and faces out of the top and bottom sixth of the 1920 pixel frame. Your profile row sits over the top of the image and the reply bar sits over the bottom.","Sharpen slightly after downscaling if the image has fine detail. Scaling a 6000 pixel photo to 1080 softens edges, and Instagram compression softens them again."],faqs:[{question:"What size should an Instagram post be?",answer:"For the feed, 1080 x 1350 pixels at 4:5. Square at 1080 x 1080 and landscape at 1080 x 566 are both accepted, but portrait takes the most vertical space in the feed. Stories and Reel covers want 1080 x 1920 at 9:16, and carousel slides use the same 1080 x 1350 as a single feed image."},{question:"Is my image uploaded to Instagram or to you when I resize it?",answer:"Neither. The resize happens entirely in this tab using canvas, and the result goes to your downloads folder. Nothing reaches our servers and nothing reaches Instagram until you post the file yourself."},{question:"Why does my iPhone photo fail to load here?",answer:"iPhones save HEIC by default, and browsers other than Safari cannot decode that format, so there is nothing for the tool to draw. Share the photo as a JPEG copy from the Photos app, or switch Settings, Camera, Formats to Most Compatible before you shoot."},{question:"Does resizing strip the location data from my photo?",answer:"Yes. The resized file is drawn into a new canvas and encoded from scratch, so it carries no EXIF metadata: no GPS coordinates, no camera model, no timestamp. If you post straight from your camera roll instead, that metadata travels with the file."},{question:"Will resizing make my Instagram photo look worse?",answer:"Scaling down to 1080 wide is what Instagram would do to your file anyway, so doing it yourself at a controlled quality usually looks better than letting the app do it. Keep the JPEG quality slider at 0.85 or above, and use PNG only when the image is a flat graphic rather than a photograph."},{question:"Why does Instagram crop my photo even at the right size?",answer:"Two common causes. In a carousel, every slide is cropped to the ratio of the first slide, so the set has to be consistent. And in a Story, a 4:5 image is placed on a 9:16 canvas, which means either bars around it or a crop. Export the Story version at 1080 x 1920 rather than reusing the feed file."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-image-resizer","instagram-video-size-checker","instagram-character-counter","instagram-caption-spacer"]},"facebook-image-resizer":{slug:"facebook-image-resizer",family:"image-resizer",platform:"facebook",datePublished:U,lastUpdated:U,label:"Facebook image resizer",metaTitle:"Facebook Image Resizer: 1200 x 630 and 1080 x 1920",metaDescription:"Resize an image for Facebook: 1200 x 630 for the feed at 1.91:1 and 1080 x 1920 for a Story, under the 4 MB cap. Free, in your browser, nothing uploaded.",heroEyebrow:"Free Facebook tool",heroTitle:"Facebook Image Resizer",heroLead:"Crop to the 1200 x 630 the Facebook feed is built around, or to 1080 x 1920 for a Story, and stay under the 4 MB ceiling that rejects more Facebook uploads than anything else.",intro:["Facebook renders a shared link preview at 1.91:1, which is where 1200 x 630 comes from, and it keeps whatever ratio you upload for a photo post. The result is that the same file can appear in two completely different shapes depending on how it is posted, and the only way to control that is to export the frame you actually want.","The number that catches people out is not the resolution, it is the 4 MB file size cap on a post image. That is the lowest ceiling of any network here, less than half of Instagram at 8 MB and a fifth of Pinterest at 20 MB, and a detailed PNG at 1200 x 630 goes past it without trying.","This resizer crops to the frame you pick, keeps the subject where you point it, and gives you a quality slider so you can land under 4 MB deliberately rather than by trial and error. It runs in your browser and nothing is uploaded."],tips:["Export at 1200 x 630 for anything that will be seen as a link preview. That is exactly 1.91:1, and it is the frame Facebook designed the feed card around.","Watch the 4 MB cap. JPEG at quality 0.85 keeps a 1200 x 630 photo well under 1 MB; the same image as PNG can be four or five times that and get rejected outright.","A photo post keeps your aspect ratio, so a portrait image really does take more vertical space in the feed. Use that deliberately rather than defaulting to landscape.","Stories are 1080 x 1920 and get cropped hard on tall phones. Keep anything important away from the outer 10 percent of the frame on every side.","Do not reuse the same file for the feed and for a Story. 1.91:1 placed on a 9:16 canvas leaves two thirds of the screen empty or forces a crop that loses the sides.","If you also publish the post to Instagram, export twice. Facebook is happiest landscape at 1200 x 630 and Instagram is happiest portrait at 1080 x 1350, and neither is a good substitute for the other."],faqs:[{question:"What size should a Facebook post image be?",answer:"For the feed and for link previews, 1200 x 630 pixels at 1.91:1, under a 4 MB file size cap. For a Story, 1080 x 1920 at 9:16, where the cap is a much more generous 100 MB. Photo posts accept any ratio between roughly 9:16 and 1.91:1."},{question:"Is my image uploaded when I use this resizer?",answer:"No. The file is decoded and redrawn in your browser with canvas, and the resized copy goes straight to your downloads. Nothing is sent to our servers and nothing is sent to Facebook until you post it yourself."},{question:"Why does my HEIC photo not open in this tool?",answer:"HEIC is the format iPhones save by default and most browsers cannot decode it, so the tool has no pixels to work with. Export a JPEG copy from Photos, or set Settings, Camera, Formats to Most Compatible. Facebook itself only accepts JPG and PNG for a post image anyway."},{question:"Does resizing remove EXIF and GPS data?",answer:"Yes. The output is encoded from a fresh canvas, so it carries no EXIF block: location, camera model and timestamp are all dropped. Facebook strips most metadata on its side too, but the resized file is already clean before it leaves your machine."},{question:"Will the quality drop when I resize for Facebook?",answer:"Some, because Facebook re-compresses uploads regardless. Give it a clean, correctly sized JPEG at quality 0.85 or higher and the result holds up well. Uploading something enormous does not help: it just means your file is compressed twice, once by you and once by Facebook."},{question:"Why does Facebook keep rejecting my image?",answer:"The 4 MB post image cap is by far the most common reason. A large PNG or a full resolution camera JPEG will exceed it. Resize to 1200 x 630, export as JPEG, and the file will typically land between 150 KB and 800 KB."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-image-resizer","facebook-video-size-checker","facebook-character-counter","instagram-image-resizer"]},"threads-image-resizer":{slug:"threads-image-resizer",family:"image-resizer",platform:"threads",datePublished:U,lastUpdated:U,label:"Threads image resizer",metaTitle:"Threads Image Resizer: Crop to 1080 x 1350",metaDescription:"Resize an image for Threads: 1080 x 1350 at 4:5 for a post or a carousel of up to 20 items, under the 8 MB cap. Free, browser only, nothing uploaded.",heroEyebrow:"Free Threads tool",heroTitle:"Threads Image Resizer",heroLead:"Threads scales your image to fit instead of cropping it, so 1080 x 1350 at 4:5 is about taking up the most room in the feed rather than about avoiding a bad crop.",intro:["Threads is the most forgiving network here for image shape. It accepts anything from a thin banner to a very tall screenshot and scales it to fit the post rather than cutting into it, so nothing you upload gets silently cropped the way it would on Instagram or X.","That changes what resizing is for. On Threads it is not damage control, it is claiming space: a 4:5 image at 1080 x 1350 occupies far more of a scrolling screen than the same picture landscape, and the feed rewards whatever stops the thumb. The 8 MB cap and the 20 item carousel are the other two numbers worth knowing.","The other reason to resize here is legibility. Screenshots of text are the format that travels furthest on Threads, and a full resolution screenshot squeezed into a post is unreadable while a deliberately cropped 1080 x 1350 version of the same thing is not. Everything runs in your browser and nothing is uploaded."],tips:["Export at 1080 x 1350. Threads will not crop you, but 4:5 is the shape that takes the most vertical space in the feed, which is the whole advantage.","Crop screenshots down to the part that matters instead of posting the full screen. A readable quote beats an accurate but tiny reproduction of your entire display.","Use PNG for screenshots and JPEG for photographs. Text on a flat background picks up visible JPEG artifacts around every letter, and the 8 MB cap gives a 1080 pixel PNG plenty of room.","Each carousel item keeps its own aspect ratio on Threads, unlike Instagram. You can mix a portrait photo and a square graphic in one post without either being cut.","A carousel holds up to 20 items, double what Instagram allows. That is enough for a genuine sequence, but the drop-off after the first few is steep, so lead with the strongest frame.","Anything wider than 3840 pixels gets downscaled during processing, so exporting above 4K is upload time you do not get back."],faqs:[{question:"What size should a Threads image be?",answer:"Threads recommends 1080 x 1350 at 4:5 and caps a post image at 8 MB. It accepts almost any ratio though, from about 1:10 to 10:1, and scales the image to fit the post rather than cropping it, so the recommendation is about how much feed space you occupy rather than about what is allowed."},{question:"Is my image uploaded anywhere by this tool?",answer:"No. The photo is decoded and redrawn with canvas inside your browser and the result is saved locally. Nothing is sent to us, and nothing reaches Threads until you post it yourself."},{question:"Why can this tool not open my iPhone photo?",answer:"It is saved as HEIC, which most browsers outside Safari cannot decode. Share a JPEG copy from the Photos app, or switch Settings, Camera, Formats to Most Compatible so your camera saves JPEG in the first place."},{question:"Does resizing strip location data from the file?",answer:"Yes. Re-encoding from a canvas produces a file with no EXIF metadata at all, so GPS coordinates, camera details and timestamps do not survive the resize. Screenshots rarely carry that data to begin with, but camera photos do."},{question:"Will the image lose quality when I resize it?",answer:"Scaling down is gentle. The JPEG encode is where quality is spent, so keep the slider at 0.85 or higher, and pick PNG for screenshots and graphics where the encode is lossless and text stays crisp."},{question:"Can I use the same image on Threads and Instagram?",answer:"Usually yes, because both recommend 1080 x 1350. The difference is in a carousel: Instagram forces every slide into the ratio of the first one, while Threads lets each item keep its own shape. Export a consistent set if the same carousel is going to both."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-image-resizer","threads-video-size-checker","threads-character-counter","instagram-image-resizer"]},"linkedin-image-resizer":{slug:"linkedin-image-resizer",family:"i