ay well inside the 10 MB cap and export as JPEG for photos. PNG is the right pick for charts, diagrams and anything with typography, and 1200 pixels wide leaves plenty of headroom.","Keep important content away from the edges of a 1.91:1 crop. Preview cards in other surfaces, including email digests, re-crop closer to square.","A multi image post takes 2 to 20 images at 10 MB each. Export them all at the same size so the set reads as one thing rather than as a pile of screenshots."],faqs:[{question:"What size should a LinkedIn post image be?",answer:"For a single image post or a link preview, 1200 x 627 pixels at 1.91:1. For a multi image post, 1200 x 1200 square. Both are capped at 10 MB per file, and LinkedIn accepts anything between 1:2.4 and 2.4:1."},{question:"Is my image uploaded when I resize it here?",answer:"No. Decoding and cropping happen in your browser with canvas, and the file lands in your downloads folder. Nothing is sent to our servers, and nothing reaches LinkedIn until you post it."},{question:"Why does my iPhone photo fail to load?",answer:"HEIC, the default iPhone format, cannot be decoded by most browsers. Export a JPEG copy from Photos or set Settings, Camera, Formats to Most Compatible. LinkedIn accepts JPG, PNG and GIF for a post image, so a HEIC file would not upload there either."},{question:"Does resizing strip location and camera data?",answer:"Yes. The resized file is written from a fresh canvas, so it has no EXIF block: no GPS coordinates, no camera serial number, no timestamp. That is worth knowing if you are posting photos taken at an office or an event."},{question:"Will resizing reduce image quality on LinkedIn?",answer:"Only marginally if you keep the JPEG quality slider at 0.85 or above. For charts, slides and anything with text, choose PNG instead: it is lossless, and the 10 MB cap means file size is rarely the constraint at 1200 pixels wide."},{question:"What about the LinkedIn carousel format?",answer:"The swipeable carousel is a document post: a PDF of up to 300 pages and 100 MB, built in a slide tool rather than assembled from images. A multi image post is the image equivalent, taking 2 to 20 files, and 1200 x 1200 is the size to export those at."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-image-resizer","linkedin-video-size-checker","linkedin-character-counter","facebook-image-resizer"]},"twitter-image-resizer":{slug:"twitter-image-resizer",family:"image-resizer",platform:"twitter",datePublished:U,lastUpdated:U,label:"X image resizer",metaTitle:"X (Twitter) Image Resizer: Crop to 1600 x 900",metaDescription:"Resize an image for X: crop to 1600 x 900 at 16:9 so the timeline does not cut it for you. Under the 5 MB cap, free, in your browser, no upload.",heroEyebrow:"Free X tool",heroTitle:"X (Twitter) Image Resizer",heroLead:"The X timeline crops every image to 16:9 until someone taps it, so 1600 x 900 is the only size where what people see is what you posted.",intro:["X recommends 1600 x 900 and, more importantly, crops the timeline preview to 16:9 whether or not your image is that shape. Post a tall screenshot and the feed shows a horizontal band from the middle of it. Post a wide panorama and it gets cut at the sides. Only a tap opens the full picture, and most people never tap.","That makes the crop decision the whole job on this network. Deciding which 16:9 slice of your image the timeline shows is more consequential here than on any other platform, which is why the focus grid matters: pick the top of the frame and a headline stays visible, pick the center and it may not.","Files are capped at 5 MB for a post image, with animated GIFs on their own budget of 15 MB from the web and 5 MB from the mobile apps. Up to four images go in a single post. Everything here runs in your browser, and nothing is uploaded."],tips:["Export at 1600 x 900. The timeline crops to 16:9 regardless, so matching it means you choose the crop instead of X choosing it.","Put the important part of the image in the middle band if you are unsure. When a non-16:9 image is auto-cropped, the center is what survives.","Screenshots of text need care. A phone screenshot is roughly 9:19.5, and the timeline will show a thin horizontal slice of it, so crop to the quote you actually want read.","Keep files under 5 MB. A 1600 x 900 JPEG at quality 0.85 lands well under 1 MB, so this cap only bites with large PNG exports.","Four images in one post are laid out in a grid and each gets its own crop. Export them at the same ratio or the grid looks uneven.","Images cost nothing against the 280 character limit, so a screenshot is often the cheapest way to add detail that will not fit in the text."],faqs:[{question:"What size should an image be on X?",answer:"1600 x 900 pixels at 16:9, under a 5 MB cap. X accepts anything from 1:3 to 3:1, but the timeline preview is cropped to 16:9 until a reader taps the image, so anything else is shown as a slice of itself."},{question:"Is my image uploaded to your servers?",answer:"No. The file is decoded and redrawn with canvas in this tab, and the resized copy goes to your downloads folder. Nothing is transmitted anywhere, and there is no account or email step."},{question:"Why can this tool not read my iPhone photo?",answer:"It is a HEIC file, which browsers other than Safari cannot decode. Export a JPEG copy from Photos, or set Settings, Camera, Formats to Most Compatible. X accepts JPG, PNG and WEBP, so HEIC would be rejected there as well."},{question:"Does resizing remove GPS and EXIF data?",answer:"Yes. The exported file is encoded from a canvas, so the EXIF block does not survive: no location, no camera model, no timestamp. X strips metadata on upload too, but the resized copy on your machine is already clean."},{question:"Will resizing hurt image quality on X?",answer:"Not much if you export at 1600 x 900 with JPEG quality 0.85 or higher. X re-compresses uploads, so handing it a clean, correctly sized file gives a better result than uploading a huge original and letting the platform downscale it."},{question:"Why does X crop my image in the timeline?",answer:"Because the timeline preview is a fixed 16:9 frame. Anything taller or wider is cropped to fit it, with the center favored, and the full image only appears when someone taps. Exporting at 1600 x 900 removes the surprise entirely."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-image-resizer","twitter-video-size-checker","twitter-character-counter","twitter-thread-maker"]},"tiktok-image-resizer":{slug:"tiktok-image-resizer",family:"image-resizer",platform:"tiktok",datePublished:U,lastUpdated:U,label:"TikTok photo resizer",metaTitle:"TikTok Photo Resizer: Crop to 1080 x 1920",metaDescription:"Resize a photo for TikTok: crop to 1080 x 1920 at 9:16 for a photo post, exported as JPEG since TikTok does not accept PNG. Free, browser only.",heroEyebrow:"Free TikTok tool",heroTitle:"TikTok Photo Resizer",heroLead:"Crop a photo to the 1080 x 1920 that a TikTok photo post expects, with room for the caption and the action rail that sit over the bottom and the right edge of every frame.",intro:["A TikTok photo post is full screen and vertical: 1080 x 1920 at 9:16, up to 35 images per post, and the first one becomes the cover. Anything less tall than 9:16 is either letterboxed or cropped, and on a surface where the image is the entire screen that gap is very visible.","There is a format trap here worth knowing before you export. The TikTok publishing API accepts JPEG and WEBP but not PNG, so a PNG export that works everywhere else will be refused when you schedule it. Pick JPEG in the format toggle below and the problem disappears. The file cap is a generous 20 MB, tied with Pinterest for the most room of any network here.","The other constraint is layout rather than pixels. The caption, the sound label and the action rail cover the bottom sixth and the right edge of the frame, so a photo with its subject in the bottom right corner is partly hidden behind the interface. Use the focus grid to move the crop before you export. Nothing is uploaded: the whole thing runs in your browser."],tips:["Export at 1080 x 1920 at 9:16. TikTok photo posts are full screen, so anything shorter leaves bars or gets cropped.","Choose JPEG, not PNG. The publishing API accepts JPEG and WEBP only, so a PNG file fails at schedule time even though it looks fine on your desktop.","Keep the subject out of the bottom sixth and the right edge. That is where the caption, the sound label and the like, comment and share buttons sit.","The first image is the cover and does most of the work. Crop it as if it were a thumbnail rather than as the first page of a sequence.","The 20 MB cap is generous, so there is no reason to over-compress. Quality 0.9 on a 1080 x 1920 JPEG is still only a couple of megabytes.","TikTok compresses on upload. A clean 1080 x 1920 export survives that far better than a 4K file that has to be crunched twice."],faqs:[{question:"What size should a TikTok photo be?",answer:"1080 x 1920 pixels at 9:16, the same full screen vertical frame TikTok uses for video. A photo post carries up to 35 images with a 20 MB cap per file, and the first image becomes the cover."},{question:"Is my photo uploaded anywhere by this tool?",answer:"No. Everything happens in your browser using canvas, and the resized file goes straight to your downloads. Nothing is sent to our servers, and nothing reaches TikTok until you post it yourself."},{question:"Why does my iPhone photo not open?",answer:"iPhones save HEIC by default and most browsers cannot decode it, so there is nothing to draw. Export a JPEG copy from Photos, or set Settings, Camera, Formats to Most Compatible. That also solves the TikTok side, since it does not accept HEIC either."},{question:"Does resizing strip the location data from my photo?",answer:"Yes. The output is encoded from a fresh canvas with no EXIF block, so GPS coordinates, camera model and timestamps are all dropped. On a platform where photo posts often come straight from a camera roll, that is a meaningful difference."},{question:"Will the photo lose quality on TikTok?",answer:"TikTok compresses hard on upload, so start clean: 1080 x 1920 at JPEG quality 0.85 to 0.9. Uploading a 4K original does not protect the detail, it just means the file is compressed twice before anyone sees it."},{question:"Why was my PNG rejected by TikTok?",answer:"The content posting API accepts JPEG and WEBP for photo posts, not PNG. Convert before scheduling: pick JPEG in the format toggle here and the export is already in an accepted format."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-image-resizer","tiktok-video-size-checker","tiktok-character-counter","instagram-image-resizer"]},"youtube-image-resizer":{slug:"youtube-image-resizer",family:"image-resizer",platform:"youtube",datePublished:U,lastUpdated:U,label:"YouTube thumbnail resizer",metaTitle:"YouTube Thumbnail Resizer: 1280 x 720 Under 2 MB",metaDescription:"Resize a YouTube thumbnail to 1280 x 720 at 16:9 and keep it under the 2 MB cap. Crop with a focus point, export as JPEG, free and in your browser.",heroEyebrow:"Free YouTube tool",heroTitle:"YouTube Thumbnail Resizer",heroLead:"Crop any image to the 1280 x 720 a YouTube thumbnail has to be, keep it inside the tight 2 MB ceiling, and check it still reads at the 210 pixels wide it gets in search.",intro:["A YouTube thumbnail is 1280 x 720 at 16:9, at least 640 pixels wide, and under 2 MB. That last number is the one that trips people up: 2 MB is a genuinely tight budget for a busy thumbnail with a face, a background and large text, and it is why YouTube recommends exporting JPEG at around quality 80 rather than PNG.","The other thing worth internalizing is how small it gets. Search results render a thumbnail as narrow as 210 pixels, roughly a sixth of the size you designed it at. Text that looks balanced at full size becomes unreadable, and a face that fills a quarter of the frame becomes a smudge. Crop tighter than feels comfortable.","This resizer takes whatever you have, a frame grab, a photo, an export from a design tool, and gives you the exact 1280 x 720 frame with a focus point you control. Use the quality slider to land under 2 MB deliberately. It runs entirely in your browser and nothing is uploaded."],tips:["Export at exactly 1280 x 720. Larger is not better here: the 2 MB cap is what actually constrains you, and YouTube re-encodes the thumbnail anyway.","Use JPEG at quality 0.8 to 0.85 rather than PNG. A detailed PNG at 1280 x 720 will often exceed 2 MB on its own, and the visible difference on a photographic thumbnail is close to nothing.","Check the design at 210 pixels wide before you commit. That is what search shows, and it is the real test of whether your text and your subject survive.","Three or four words maximum on the thumbnail, set very large. The video title sits right next to it, so the thumbnail does not need to repeat the title.","Keep the bottom right corner clear. The duration badge is stamped over it in every surface YouTube renders.","Crop faces larger than feels right on a big screen. A face that fills a third of the frame at 1280 x 720 is still small at thumbnail size."],faqs:[{question:"What size should a YouTube thumbnail be?",answer:"1280 x 720 pixels at 16:9, with a minimum width of 640 pixels and a hard file size cap of 2 MB. JPG, PNG and GIF are accepted. YouTube displays the same image everywhere from a full width home page card down to roughly 210 pixels wide in search."},{question:"Is my thumbnail uploaded to you when I resize it?",answer:"No. The image is decoded and redrawn with canvas inside your browser and the result is saved locally. Nothing is sent to our servers, and nothing reaches YouTube until you set the thumbnail in Studio yourself."},{question:"Why does my iPhone screenshot or photo fail to load?",answer:"If it is a HEIC file, most browsers cannot decode it and the tool has nothing to draw. Export a JPEG copy from Photos, or switch Settings, Camera, Formats to Most Compatible. YouTube does not accept HEIC thumbnails either, so the conversion is needed regardless."},{question:"Does resizing remove metadata from the image?",answer:"Yes. Re-encoding from a canvas produces a file with no EXIF block, so GPS coordinates, camera model and timestamps are all stripped. If your thumbnail is a photograph taken at home, that is one less thing published with the video."},{question:"Will the quality drop if I resize to 1280 x 720?",answer:"Barely, and the 2 MB cap makes some compression necessary anyway. Quality 0.8 to 0.85 is the range YouTube itself recommends for thumbnails. Below about 0.7 you start to see blocking in flat backgrounds, which is very visible on a thumbnail with large areas of solid color."},{question:"Why does YouTube reject my thumbnail?",answer:"Almost always the 2 MB limit, usually because the file is a PNG. Re-export as JPEG at quality 0.8 and a 1280 x 720 thumbnail typically lands between 150 KB and 500 KB. The other cause is being under the 640 pixel minimum width."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-image-resizer","youtube-video-size-checker","youtube-character-counter","pinterest-image-resizer"]},"pinterest-image-resizer":{slug:"pinterest-image-resizer",family:"image-resizer",platform:"pinterest",datePublished:U,lastUpdated:U,label:"Pinterest pin resizer",metaTitle:"Pinterest Pin Resizer: Crop to 1000 x 1500 (2:3)",metaDescription:"Resize an image into a Pin: crop to 1000 x 1500 at 2:3, the ratio Pinterest builds its feed around. Free, runs in your browser, nothing uploaded.",heroEyebrow:"Free Pinterest tool",heroTitle:"Pinterest Pin Resizer",heroLead:"Pinterest designs its entire feed around 2:3, so 1000 x 1500 is the one size where a Pin is shown whole. Taller Pins are truncated rather than scaled down.",intro:["Pinterest is stricter about shape than it looks. A standard Pin should be 1000 x 1500 at 2:3, and while the feed accepts other ratios, anything meaningfully taller is truncated in the grid rather than shrunk to fit. A 1000 x 3000 infographic does not appear as a long thin Pin, it appears as the top half of one.","On the generous side, the file cap is 20 MB, tied with TikTok photo posts for the most room of any network here. There is genuinely no reason to over-compress a Pin: the surface rewards detail, people zoom into Pins far more than they zoom into feed posts, and a crisp 1000 x 1500 JPEG at high quality is only a few hundred kilobytes.","This resizer crops to 2:3 with a focus point you choose, so a landscape photograph becomes a proper vertical Pin around the subject rather than around the middle of the frame. It runs in your browser and nothing is uploaded."],tips:["Export at 1000 x 1500. Two by three is the ratio the grid is laid out for, and it is the only shape guaranteed to be shown complete.","Do not go taller to get more space. Pinterest truncates rather than scales, so the bottom of a very tall Pin is simply not seen in the feed.","Use the 20 MB cap. Quality 0.9 or higher is worth it here because Pins get zoomed and saved rather than scrolled past, and there is plenty of room in the budget.","Put any text in the top third. That is the part guaranteed to be visible in the grid, and it is what someone reads before deciding to open the Pin.","Keep a margin inside the frame. Pins are rendered with rounded corners, so content pushed right to the edge gets clipped at the corners.","Turn a landscape photo into a vertical Pin deliberately, not by accident. Use the focus grid so the crop lands on the subject rather than on the middle of a wide shot."],faqs:[{question:"What size should a Pinterest Pin be?",answer:"1000 x 1500 pixels at 2:3, under a 20 MB file cap, as a JPG or PNG. Pinterest accepts ratios from about 1:2 up to 1.91:1, but 2:3 is what the feed is designed around, and taller Pins are truncated in the grid rather than scaled to fit."},{question:"Is my image uploaded when I use this Pin resizer?",answer:"No. The file is decoded and redrawn with canvas inside your browser, and the resized Pin goes straight to your downloads folder. Nothing is sent to our servers, and nothing reaches Pinterest until you upload it yourself."},{question:"Why can this tool not open my iPhone photo?",answer:"HEIC, the default iPhone format, cannot be decoded by most browsers. Export a JPEG copy from the Photos app, or set Settings, Camera, Formats to Most Compatible. Pinterest accepts JPG and PNG for a standard Pin, so HEIC would not upload there either."},{question:"Does resizing strip location data from the image?",answer:"Yes. The Pin is encoded from a fresh canvas, so it carries no EXIF metadata: no GPS coordinates, no camera model, no timestamp. Pins of homes, studios and workshops are common on Pinterest, which makes that worth knowing."},{question:"Will resizing reduce the quality of my Pin?",answer:"Very little at 1000 x 1500 with the quality slider at 0.9. Pinterest gives you 20 MB, so this is the one network where erring toward higher quality costs nothing. Use PNG for graphics with flat color and text, JPEG for photographs."},{question:"Can I use a square or landscape image as a Pin?",answer:"You can, and it will publish, but it takes up much less of the vertical feed and performs accordingly. Crop it to 2:3 with the focus point on the subject instead: a 1000 x 1500 Pin made from a landscape photo occupies more than twice the screen height of the original shape."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-image-resizer","pinterest-video-size-checker","pinterest-character-counter","instagram-image-resizer"]},"social-media-post-preview":{slug:"social-media-post-preview",family:"post-preview",platform:null,datePublished:G,lastUpdated:G,label:"Social media post preview",metaTitle:"Social Media Post Preview and Mockup Generator",metaDescription:"Free post mockup generator: paste a caption and see where Instagram, LinkedIn, TikTok and 5 more networks fold it. Runs in your browser, nothing uploaded.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"Social Media Post Preview",heroLead:"Paste one caption and see it laid out the way each of the 8 networks lays it out, with the fold drawn where that network stops showing your words.",intro:['Every network cuts a caption, and they cut it far earlier than the limit suggests. Pinterest shows about 50 characters of a description in the feed. TikTok shows roughly 100 over the video. Instagram folds at about 125 behind "... more", YouTube at 157, LinkedIn at 210 on desktop and closer to 140 on a phone, Facebook at about 477. Two networks do not fold at all: X stops a standard post at 280 and refuses more, and Threads shows all 500 characters it allows.',"That means the number that decides whether your caption works is almost never the caption limit. It is the fold, and it is small enough that most captions get judged on their first sentence alone. This preview draws that line for you: type once, switch networks, and watch how much of the same paragraph each one is willing to show.","The card is a neutral mockup rather than a screenshot. It reproduces the three things that actually change where your words get cut, the shape of the media box, how the name line reads, and where the fold lands, without reproducing an interface that is not ours. Everything runs in your browser: the caption is never sent anywhere, and an image you add is shown from a local URL and nothing more."],tips:["Write the first sentence for the fold, not for the caption. On six of these eight networks it is the only part a scrolling reader is guaranteed to see, so it has to make sense with nothing after it.","Front-load the hook and back-load the context. Background, credits, links and disclaimers all read fine below the fold. A claim does not, because nobody taps to find out what you were claiming.","Put hashtags at the end, always. They work identically wherever they sit in the caption, and a tag block at the top spends your most valuable 50 to 125 characters on text that reads as noise.","Check the same caption on the tightest network you publish to. If it survives Pinterest at 50 characters and TikTok at 100, it survives everywhere else without further edits.","Do not reuse one caption across all 8 networks unchanged. The folds are between 50 and 477 characters apart, so a caption tuned for LinkedIn arrives on TikTok with the useful part hidden.","Leave yourself margin. The fold moves with screen width, text size and language, so a caption that lands exactly on the number on your laptop lands past it on a narrower phone."],faqs:[{question:"Is this a real screenshot of the network?",answer:"No, and it is not trying to be. It is an HTML approximation built from the published layout numbers: the media aspect ratio each network recommends, the way the name and handle line reads, and the point where the caption gets folded. There are no platform logos, colors or interface elements in the card, because copying those would make it look more accurate than it is."},{question:"Will the preview match what I see on my phone exactly?",answer:"Not to the character. Every fold on this page moves with screen width, with the text size a reader has chosen and with the language of the caption. Instagram folds at roughly 125 characters, LinkedIn cuts at about 210 on desktop but closer to 140 on mobile, and the TikTok cut depends on how wide the screen is. Write under the number rather than up to it and the difference stops mattering."},{question:"Where does my uploaded photo go?",answer:"Nowhere. The image is turned into a local object URL and drawn straight into the card inside the tab you are reading. It is not uploaded to us, it is not sent to any network, and the URL is released the moment you replace the image or close the page."},{question:"Can I download the mockup as an image?",answer:"Not yet. Copying the caption is one button, but exporting the card as a picture is not built. For now, a screenshot does the job: on macOS press Cmd, Shift and 4 then drag around the card, and on Windows press the Windows key, Shift and S. Expand or fold the caption first so the version you capture is the one you meant."},{question:"Which networks fold a caption and which do not?",answer:"Six fold: Pinterest at about 50 characters, TikTok at 100, Instagram at 125, YouTube at 157, LinkedIn at 210 on desktop, Facebook at about 477. Two do not: X never folds a standard post because 280 characters is a hard stop rather than a preview, and Threads shows the whole of its 500 character post."},{question:"Do I need an account to use this?",answer:"No. There is no signup, no email step and no connected account. The tool is plain JavaScript running in your browser, and closing the tab is all the cleanup there is."}],relatedToolSlugs:["instagram-post-preview","linkedin-post-preview","twitter-post-preview","social-media-character-counter"]},"instagram-post-preview":{slug:"instagram-post-preview",family:"post-preview",platform:"instagram",datePublished:G,lastUpdated:G,label:"Instagram post preview",metaTitle:"Instagram Post Preview: See Where the Caption Folds",metaDescription:'Free Instagram caption preview: see your caption in a 4:5 feed mockup with the "... more" fold drawn at 125 characters. Browser only, nothing uploaded.',heroEyebrow:"Free Instagram tool",heroTitle:"Instagram Post Preview",heroLead:"Instagram gives you 2,200 characters and shows about 125 of them. This preview draws that line so you can see which half of your caption people actually read.",intro:['The Instagram feed collapses a caption after roughly 125 characters behind a "... more" link. That is about one and a half sentences. Everything else, the context, the story, the call to action, the hashtags, sits behind a tap that most of the feed never makes, which is why the opening line is the caption as far as reach is concerned.',"The gap between what you are allowed and what is shown is the widest on this network of any here: 2,200 characters permitted, roughly 125 displayed. It is easy to write to the ceiling and never notice that seventeen eighteenths of the caption is folded away.",'Paste your caption below and the card shows it in the 4:5 shape Instagram builds the feed around, cut where the fold falls, with the "... more" control drawn in place. Toggle to the expanded view to read the whole thing, add your own image if you want to see it in context, and nothing you type or upload leaves the browser.'],tips:["Treat the first 125 characters as the whole caption. Write it so it works with nothing after it, then add the rest for the people who tap.",'Do not open with a greeting or an emoji run. "Happy Monday everyone!" spends a fifth of the visible caption saying nothing, and it is the fifth people read first.',"Keep hashtags out of the opening. All 30 work exactly the same at the end of the caption, and a tag block at the top pushes your actual sentence below the fold.","Use line breaks deliberately. Instagram strips trailing whitespace and drops empty lines, so gaps you drafted in the composer collapse on publish unless you space the caption properly first.",'Ask a question or make a claim before the cut. The two openings that reliably earn the "... more" tap are an unfinished thought and a statement someone wants to argue with.',"Remember the caption is not the hook on a Reel. On video the first second of footage does that job, and the caption is the follow-up rather than the entry point."],faqs:[{question:"Is this a real Instagram screenshot?",answer:"No. It is an HTML approximation of a feed post built from published numbers: a 4:5 media box because Instagram recommends 1080 x 1350, a username led identity line, and the caption folded at the point Instagram folds it. There is no Instagram branding or interface in the card, because a neutral mockup is honest about being an approximation."},{question:"Will my caption fold in exactly the same place on my phone?",answer:'Close, but not to the character. The feed collapses a caption after roughly 125 characters behind a "... more" link, and that point shifts with screen width, with the text size your reader has chosen and with the language. Write the opening so it works comfortably under 125 rather than exactly at it.'},{question:"Where does the photo I upload go?",answer:"Nowhere. It is turned into a local object URL and drawn into the card in your browser. Nothing is uploaded to us and nothing reaches Instagram until you post it yourself. The URL is released as soon as you replace the image or leave the page."},{question:"Can I download this preview as an image?",answer:"Not yet. You can copy the caption with one button, but exporting the card as a picture is not built. A screenshot works well in the meantime: Cmd, Shift and 4 on macOS, or the Windows key with Shift and S on Windows. Set the card to folded or expanded first so you capture the version you want."},{question:'How many characters show before "... more" on Instagram?',answer:"About 125, though the exact point depends on screen width and text size. The hard caption limit is 2,200 characters including hashtags, mentions, emoji and line breaks, so the visible portion is roughly one eighteenth of what you are allowed to write."},{question:"Does the fold work the same on a Reel or a carousel?",answer:'The caption behaves the same way in all three: roughly the first line, then "... more". What changes is how much attention the caption gets at all. On a Reel the video carries the hook and the caption supports it, while on a single feed image the caption often is the hook.'}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-post-preview","instagram-character-counter","instagram-caption-spacer","instagram-image-resizer"]},"facebook-post-preview":{slug:"facebook-post-preview",family:"post-preview",platform:"facebook",datePublished:G,lastUpdated:G,label:"Facebook post mockup",metaTitle:"Facebook Post Mockup and Preview Tool",metaDescription:'Free Facebook post mockup: preview your post in a 1.91:1 card and see the "See more" fold at about 477 characters. Runs in your browser, no upload.',heroEyebrow:"Free Facebook tool",heroTitle:"Facebook Post Mockup",heroLead:'Facebook folds a post behind "See more" after about 477 characters on desktop and earlier on mobile. Build the mockup here and see the cut before you publish it.',intro:['Facebook has the most generous field on this list and the least useful ceiling: 63,206 characters is a field size, not advice. What matters is that desktop folds a post behind "See more" after about 477 characters, and mobile folds earlier depending on screen width, so the practical limit is a few hundred characters rather than sixty thousand.','The engagement data pushes in the same direction and much harder. Short posts of roughly 40 to 80 characters consistently earn more engagement than long ones, which means the fold is rarely the thing that stops you: most Facebook posts should end long before anyone would need to tap "See more" at all.',"This mockup shows your post in the 1.91:1 shape the feed card is built around, with a page style name line and the fold drawn in place. Paste the copy, add an image if you have one, and see the whole thing laid out. Nothing you type or upload leaves your browser."],tips:["Aim for 40 to 80 characters when engagement is the goal. Short Facebook posts consistently outperform long ones, and at that length the fold is irrelevant.",'If the post has to be long, make the first 477 characters a complete unit. Somebody who never taps "See more" should still have read something whole.',"Assume mobile folds sooner. The 477 figure is a desktop number and phones cut earlier depending on width, so treat 300 characters as the safer working ceiling.","Use 0 to 2 hashtags. Facebook is the one network here where a tag block adds nothing and looks imported from Instagram.","Export images at 1200 x 630 and keep them under 4 MB. That 4 MB cap is the lowest of any network here and the most common Facebook upload rejection.","Put links in the post body, not only in an image. A link preview renders at 1.91:1, so the same photo can appear in two different crops depending on how you post it."],faqs:[{question:"Is this a real Facebook screenshot?",answer:"No. It is a neutral HTML approximation built from published layout numbers: a 1.91:1 media box matching the 1200 x 630 feed image, a page style name line, and the caption folded where Facebook folds it. There is no Facebook branding or interface in the card."},{question:"Will the fold land in the same place on my phone?",answer:'Not exactly. Desktop folds a post behind "See more" after about 477 characters, and mobile folds earlier depending on screen width. Treat 477 as the generous end of the range and write the first 300 characters as if they were the whole post.'},{question:"Where does the image I add go?",answer:"Nowhere. It becomes a local object URL and is drawn into the card inside your browser. It is never uploaded to us and never reaches Facebook until you publish it yourself, and the URL is released when you replace the image or close the tab."},{question:"Can I download the mockup as an image?",answer:"Not yet. The caption copies with one button, but there is no export to a picture. A screenshot covers it: Cmd, Shift and 4 on macOS, or the Windows key with Shift and S on Windows. Choose folded or expanded before you capture."},{question:"How long should a Facebook post be?",answer:"Shorter than the fold, and usually far shorter. The field holds 63,206 characters and the desktop fold sits at about 477, but posts of roughly 40 to 80 characters consistently earn more engagement than anything longer. The ceiling is a technical fact, not a target."},{question:"Does a link preview change where the post folds?",answer:"No, the text folds at the same point. What the link preview changes is the layout beneath it: the card renders at 1.91:1 regardless of the shape of the image on the destination page, so a portrait image on your site still appears as a landscape crop in the feed."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-post-preview","facebook-character-counter","facebook-image-resizer","instagram-post-preview"]},"threads-post-preview":{slug:"threads-post-preview",family:"post-preview",platform:"threads",datePublished:G,lastUpdated:G,label:"Threads post preview",metaTitle:"Threads Post Preview for the 500 Character Post",metaDescription:"Free Threads post preview: see your post laid out in full, because Threads shows all 500 characters and never folds. Browser only, nothing uploaded.",heroEyebrow:"Free Threads tool",heroTitle:"Threads Post Preview",heroLead:"Threads is one of only two networks here that never folds a post: all 500 characters are shown. The preview is about pacing and shape rather than about a hidden second half.",intro:['There is no "more" control to worry about on Threads. Each post gets its own 500 characters and every one of them is displayed, so longer thoughts are split across replies rather than stretched behind a fold. That makes the preview a different exercise from the Instagram one: nothing is hidden, so the question is whether the post reads well as a block, not whether the hook survives a cut.',"What Threads does have is a hard ceiling that arrives quickly. Five hundred characters is three or four sentences. There is no classic hashtag block either: a post carries a single topic tag, and its text is charged against the same 500 characters as everything else, so a long tag is a real cost.","This preview lays the post out in the 4:5 shape Threads recommends for an image, with a name and handle line above it, and counts every character against the 500 as you type. Everything runs in your browser and nothing is uploaded."],tips:["Write to 500 and stop. There is no fold to hide behind and no expansion control, so anything you cannot fit belongs in a reply rather than in the same post.","Make the first post of a chain work alone. The feed shows the opener and the replies sit under it, so a post that only makes sense after the second one will not get anybody to the second one.","Keep the single topic tag short. Threads has no separate hashtag block, and every character of the tag comes out of the same 500.","Spend URLs carefully. Threads charges the full length of a link rather than a flat rate, so a 90 character tracking URL is nearly a fifth of the post gone.","Screenshots of text travel further here than polished graphics. Crop to the quote that matters instead of posting the whole screen, and legibility beats resolution.","Do not paste an Instagram caption straight across. A 2,200 character caption does not fit a 500 character post, and the two surfaces reward completely different lengths."],faqs:[{question:"Is this a real Threads screenshot?",answer:"No. It is a neutral HTML approximation: a 4:5 media box matching the 1080 x 1350 that Threads recommends, a name and handle line, and generic action icons. There is no Threads branding or interface in the card, only the layout facts that change how a post reads."},{question:"Will this match what I see on my phone exactly?",answer:"The text will, because nothing is folded: each post in a chain gets its own 500 characters and all of them are shown. What varies by device is line wrapping and how tall the post looks, not how much of it is visible."},{question:"Where does my uploaded image go?",answer:"Nowhere. It becomes a local object URL rendered inside your browser. It is not uploaded to us and does not reach Threads until you post it yourself, and the URL is released when you replace the image or close the tab."},{question:"Can I download this preview as an image?",answer:"Not yet. Copying the post text is one button, but there is no export to a picture. Take a screenshot instead: Cmd, Shift and 4 on macOS, or the Windows key with Shift and S on Windows."},{question:"Does Threads ever truncate a post?",answer:"Not the way Instagram or LinkedIn do. There is no published fold, because 500 characters is short enough to show whole. The limit applies to each post in a chain individually, so a six post chain gives you 3,000 characters in total with nothing hidden."},{question:"Why does the card look different from the Instagram one?",answer:"Because the two networks differ in the details that matter for a preview. Threads leads with a name and handle rather than a username, accepts almost any image shape and scales it to fit rather than cropping, and never folds the text. The card reflects those three differences and nothing else."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-post-preview","threads-character-counter","threads-post-splitter","instagram-post-preview"]},"linkedin-post-preview":{slug:"linkedin-post-preview",family:"post-preview",platform:"linkedin",datePublished:G,lastUpdated:G,label:"LinkedIn post preview",metaTitle:"LinkedIn Post Preview Before You Publish",metaDescription:'Free LinkedIn post preview: see the "...see more" cut at about 210 characters on desktop and 140 on mobile. Runs in your browser, nothing uploaded.',heroEyebrow:"Free LinkedIn tool",heroTitle:"LinkedIn Post Preview",heroLead:"LinkedIn cuts a post at about 210 characters on desktop and closer to 140 on mobile. Write for the tighter number and see exactly where it lands.",intro:['LinkedIn folds a post behind "...see more" at roughly 210 characters on desktop, and closer to 140 on mobile. Two different numbers for the same post is what makes this network awkward to write for: a hook that fits neatly on a laptop is already cut on a phone, and most of the feed is read on a phone.',"The practical answer is to write for 140 and let desktop readers get a bonus line. That is a tweet and a half to establish who you are talking to, what the post is about and why anybody should tap. The remaining 2,790 characters of the 3,000 limit are for the people who already decided to stay.",'This preview lays your post out in the 1.91:1 shape LinkedIn built the feed around, with a page style name line, and draws the "...see more" control where the fold falls. Switch between folded and expanded to check both halves. Nothing you type or upload leaves your browser.'],tips:["Write for 140 characters, not 210. Mobile folds earlier than desktop, and the tighter number is the one most of your audience sees.",'Skip the throat clearing. "I have been thinking a lot lately about..." burns the entire visible portion before the post has said anything.',"Break the first line out on its own. A single short line followed by a blank line reads cleanly at both fold points and survives either cut.","Keep hashtags to 3 to 5 and put them at the bottom. They are indexed the same wherever they sit, and at the top they eat the only lines anybody reads.","Do not bury the ask. If the post ends with a question or a link, say in the first line that it does, otherwise only the people who expand ever find it.","Export post images at 1200 x 627. That is the size LinkedIn designed the feed card and its link previews around, and square at 1200 x 1200 is worth testing against it for vertical space."],faqs:[{question:"Is this a real LinkedIn screenshot?",answer:"No. It is a neutral HTML approximation built from published layout numbers: a 1.91:1 media box matching 1200 x 627, a page style name line, and the post folded where LinkedIn folds it. There is no LinkedIn branding or interface in the card."},{question:'Will the "...see more" cut land in the same place for everyone?',answer:'No, and the gap is unusually wide here. Desktop cuts the post at about 210 characters behind "...see more", while mobile folds closer to 140, so write for the tighter number. Text size settings and language move it further, which is why the preview should be read as an approximation.'},{question:"Where does the image I upload go?",answer:"Nowhere. It is turned into a local object URL and drawn into the card in your browser. Nothing is uploaded to us and nothing reaches LinkedIn until you publish it yourself, and the URL is released when you replace the image or close the page."},{question:"Can I download the preview as an image?",answer:"Not yet. Copying the post text takes one button, but exporting the card as a picture is not built. A screenshot is the workaround: Cmd, Shift and 4 on macOS, or the Windows key with Shift and S on Windows. Fold or expand the card first so you capture the right version."},{question:'How many characters does LinkedIn show before "see more"?',answer:"About 210 on desktop and closer to 140 on mobile, out of a 3,000 character limit. That means somewhere between 5 and 7 percent of a full length post is visible in the feed, which is why the opening line does nearly all the work."},{question:"Does a document post or a carousel fold the same way?",answer:"The text does, yes. What changes is what sits under it: the swipeable carousel LinkedIn is known for is a document post, a PDF of up to 300 pages and 100 MB, rather than an image set. The caption above it is folded at the same point regardless."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-post-preview","linkedin-character-counter","linkedin-image-resizer","facebook-post-preview"]},"twitter-post-preview":{slug:"twitter-post-preview",family:"post-preview",platform:"twitter",datePublished:G,lastUpdated:G,label:"Tweet mockup",metaTitle:"Tweet Mockup and X Post Preview Tool",metaDescription:"Free tweet mockup: preview an X post in a 16:9 card with anything past 280 characters marked in red. Runs in your browser, nothing is uploaded.",heroEyebrow:"Free X tool",heroTitle:"Tweet Mockup and Preview",heroLead:"A standard X post never folds: 280 characters is a hard stop rather than a preview. The mockup shows the post in full and marks anything past the ceiling in red.",intro:['X is the exception on this list. Nothing is hidden behind a "more" control on a standard post, because a standard post cannot be long enough to need one: 280 characters is where the composer stops accepting text. The preview question here is not "what gets cut" but "does this fit, and does it read".','Two details change the arithmetic. Every link counts as 23 characters no matter its length, so shortening a URL before you paste it saves you nothing. And X Premium raises the ceiling to 25,000 characters, at which point posts longer than 280 do collapse behind a "Show more" toggle in the timeline, which is usually an argument for a thread instead.',"This mockup lays the post out in the 16:9 shape the timeline crops images to, with a name and handle line, and marks any tail past 280 characters in red so you can see exactly how much has to go. Nothing you type or upload leaves your browser."],tips:["Treat 280 as a design constraint rather than a limit to hit. The posts that travel are usually well under it, because a short post is easy to quote.","Count links at 23 characters. A 180 character tracking URL and a 20 character short link cost exactly the same on X, so paste whichever one you would rather a reader see.","Attach media instead of describing it. Images, videos and GIFs cost nothing against the 280, which makes a screenshot the cheapest way to add detail that will not fit.","Crop images to 16:9 at 1600 x 900. The timeline crops every image to 16:9 until somebody taps, so anything else is shown as a slice of itself.","Use 1 to 2 hashtags at most. On X a tag block reads as spam far more quickly than it does on Instagram or TikTok.",'If the idea needs more than 280 characters, build a thread rather than a Premium long post. The timeline still shows only the first 280 of a long post before "Show more", so threading keeps each idea visible.'],faqs:[{question:"Is this a real X screenshot?",answer:"No. It is a neutral HTML approximation: a 16:9 media box matching the 1600 x 900 X recommends, a name and handle line, and generic action icons. There is no X branding or interface in the card, because a mockup that copied them would claim more accuracy than it has."},{question:"Will the post look exactly like this on my phone?",answer:'The text will be complete either way, because a standard post never folds. What varies by device is line wrapping and how the image is cropped in the timeline. If you have Premium, remember that posts longer than 280 characters collapse behind a "Show more" toggle in the timeline.'},{question:"Where does the image I add go?",answer:"Nowhere. It becomes a local object URL rendered inside your browser. It is never uploaded to us and never reaches X until you post it yourself, and the URL is released when you replace the image or close the tab."},{question:"Can I download the tweet mockup as an image?",answer:"Not yet. The text copies with one button, but there is no picture export. A screenshot works: Cmd, Shift and 4 on macOS, or the Windows key with Shift and S on Windows, dragging a box around the card."},{question:"Why is part of my text red?",answer:"Because it is past 280 characters and X will not accept it on a standard account. Nothing folds on X, so the tail is not hidden text, it is text that cannot be published. Either cut it or split the post into a thread."},{question:"How are links counted here?",answer:"The preview counts characters as written. X itself rewrites every link through t.co and charges a flat 23 characters however long the original is, so a post with a very long URL may fit on X even when the raw count looks over. The character counter applies that 23 character weighting if you need the exact number."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-post-preview","twitter-character-counter","twitter-thread-maker","twitter-image-resizer"]},"tiktok-post-preview":{slug:"tiktok-post-preview",family:"post-preview",platform:"tiktok",datePublished:G,lastUpdated:G,label:"TikTok caption preview",metaTitle:"TikTok Caption Preview: What Shows Over the Video",metaDescription:'Free TikTok caption preview: see roughly the 100 characters that show over the video before the "more" toggle, in a 9:16 mockup. Browser only, no upload.',heroEyebrow:"Free TikTok tool",heroTitle:"TikTok Caption Preview",heroLead:'Only about the first line of a TikTok caption shows over the video, roughly 100 characters, before the "more" toggle. This preview draws that line in a 9:16 frame.',intro:['TikTok gives a video description 4,000 characters and displays roughly 100 of them. Only about the first line shows over the video before the "more" toggle, and the exact cut depends on screen width. That is the tightest ratio of allowed to visible on any network except Pinterest, and it means the caption is effectively a one line subtitle with an archive attached.',"The caption also competes with the video itself. It sits over the footage, alongside the sound label and the action rail, which cover the bottom sixth and the right edge of the frame. A caption that runs long does not just get folded, it covers the thing people came to watch.",'This preview lays the caption over a 9:16 frame, the shape a TikTok photo post and video both use, and cuts it where the "more" toggle appears. Add your own cover image to see the two together. Nothing you type or upload leaves your browser.'],tips:['Write one line, then stop. Roughly 100 characters is what shows, and a caption that needs the "more" tap is a caption most viewers never finish.',"Say what the video is, not what you feel about it. The visible line works hardest as a promise of payoff, because the viewer is deciding whether to keep watching, not whether to read.","Put hashtags after the sentence. Three to five is the useful range, and they are charged against the same 4,000 characters as everything else, spaces and emoji included.","Keep the subject out of the bottom sixth and the right edge of the frame. The caption, the sound label and the action buttons all sit over the video there.","The separate title field stops at 90 characters. That is shorter than the caption fold, so it is a headline rather than a summary.","Export photo posts at 1080 x 1920 as JPEG. The publishing API accepts JPEG and WEBP but not PNG, so a PNG export fails at schedule time even though it looks fine locally."],faqs:[{question:"Is this a real TikTok screenshot?",answer:"No. It is a neutral HTML approximation: a 9:16 media box matching the 1080 x 1920 TikTok expects, a name and handle line, and generic action icons. There is no TikTok branding or interface in the card, and no attempt to reproduce the app."},{question:"Will the caption cut in the same place on my phone?",answer:'Approximately. Only about the first line shows over the video, roughly 100 characters, before the "more" toggle, and the exact cut depends on screen width. A narrow phone shows less than the preview does, so keep the visible line comfortably under 100 characters.'},{question:"Where does my uploaded image go?",answer:"Nowhere. It becomes a local object URL and is drawn into the card inside your browser. It is not uploaded to us and does not reach TikTok until you post it yourself, and the URL is released when you replace it or close the tab."},{question:"Can I download this preview as an image?",answer:"Not yet. Copying the caption is one button, but exporting the card as a picture is not built. Use a screenshot: Cmd, Shift and 4 on macOS, or the Windows key with Shift and S on Windows."},{question:"How long can a TikTok caption be?",answer:'Four thousand characters, raised from the old 2,200 limit. Hashtags, mentions, emoji and spaces are all charged against that total and there is no separate hashtag cap. The number that matters is still the roughly 100 characters shown before "more".'},{question:"Does the caption matter if the video is good?",answer:"Less than it does on Instagram or LinkedIn, but it is not free. The visible line is what a viewer reads in the first second while deciding whether to swipe, and the full description is also what TikTok reads for topic and search, so the part below the fold still earns its place."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-post-preview","tiktok-character-counter","tiktok-image-resizer","instagram-post-preview"]},"youtube-post-preview":{slug:"youtube-post-preview",family:"post-preview",platform:"youtube",datePublished:G,lastUpdated:G,label:"YouTube description preview",metaTitle:"YouTube Description Preview Before Show More",metaDescription:'Free YouTube description preview: see the roughly 157 characters shown before "Show more" alongside a 16:9 thumbnail and title line. Browser only, no upload.',heroEyebrow:"Free YouTube tool",heroTitle:"YouTube Description Preview",heroLead:'The watch page shows roughly the first 157 characters of a description before "Show more", and that opening usually seeds the search snippet too. See it before you publish.',intro:['A YouTube description holds 5,000 characters and the watch page shows roughly the first 157 before "Show more". That opening does double duty: it is what a viewer reads under the title, and it usually seeds the snippet that appears in search results, so it is the one part of the description that has to be written rather than assembled.','Everything else in a description, the timestamps, the links, the affiliate disclosures, the credits, the social handles, lives below that line and is fine there. The mistake is starting with any of it. A description that opens with "Subscribe here" spends the entire visible portion and the entire search snippet on a call to action nobody searched for.','This preview shows a 16:9 thumbnail, a separate title line clipped to the 100 character title limit, and the description cut where "Show more" appears. Add your own thumbnail to see the pair together. Nothing you type or upload leaves your browser.'],tips:["Write the first 157 characters as a search snippet. It is what appears under the title on the watch page and usually what Google shows, so it should describe the video in plain language.","Move links, timestamps and disclosures below the fold. All of them work perfectly well there, and none of them belong in a search snippet.","Keep titles under 100 characters and front-load the specific words. Search and suggested feeds truncate long titles, and the distinguishing detail is what gets cut first.","Use no more than 15 hashtags. Go past 15 and YouTube ignores every hashtag on the video, not just the extras, and only the first 3 appear above the title.","Export thumbnails at 1280 x 720 and under 2 MB. That cap is tight for a busy thumbnail, which is why JPEG at around quality 80 beats PNG here.","Test the thumbnail at 210 pixels wide. That is roughly the size search results render it at, and text that looked balanced at full size disappears completely."],faqs:[{question:"Is this a real YouTube screenshot?",answer:'No. It is a neutral HTML approximation: a 16:9 thumbnail box matching the 1280 x 720 YouTube requires, a separate title line because a video card has one, and the description folded where "Show more" appears. There is no YouTube branding or interface in the card.'},{question:"Will the description cut at the same point for everyone?",answer:'Roughly. The watch page shows about the first 157 characters of the description before "Show more", and that point shifts with window width and text size. Mobile and the search snippet both show less, so treat 157 as the generous end of the range.'},{question:"Where does the thumbnail I upload go?",answer:"Nowhere. It becomes a local object URL and is drawn into the card in your browser. It is not uploaded to us and does not reach YouTube until you set it in Studio yourself, and the URL is released when you replace it or close the page."},{question:"Can I download this preview as an image?",answer:"Not yet. Copying the description takes one button, but exporting the card as a picture is not built. Take a screenshot instead: Cmd, Shift and 4 on macOS, or the Windows key with Shift and S on Windows."},{question:"How long can a YouTube title and description be?",answer:'The title stops at 100 characters and the description at 5,000. Only the first 157 characters or so of the description are shown before "Show more", and only the first 3 hashtags appear above the title.'},{question:"Does the description affect search rankings?",answer:"The opening matters more than the length. The first 157 characters seed the snippet a searcher reads, and the full description gives YouTube topic signals, but padding it with keyword lists does nothing useful. Write the opening for a person and let the rest carry the links and timestamps."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-post-preview","youtube-character-counter","youtube-image-resizer","tiktok-post-preview"]},"pinterest-post-preview":{slug:"pinterest-post-preview",family:"post-preview",platform:"pinterest",datePublished:G,lastUpdated:G,label:"Pinterest pin preview",metaTitle:"Pinterest Pin Preview: Description in the Feed",metaDescription:"Free Pinterest pin preview: only the first 50 to 60 characters of a description show under a Pin. See the cut in a 2:3 mockup, in your browser.",heroEyebrow:"Free Pinterest tool",heroTitle:"Pinterest Pin Preview",heroLead:"Only the first 50 to 60 characters of a Pin description appear under it in the feed. That is the tightest fold on any network here, and this preview draws it.",intro:["Pinterest allows a 500 character Pin description and shows about 50 to 60 of it in the feed. The rest opens on the Pin page. That is roughly a tenth of what you are allowed, and by some distance the tightest fold of any network on this site, which makes the first half sentence the whole visible description.","The saving grace is that Pinterest is a search surface rather than a scroll surface. It reads the title and the description as search text, so plain keywords beat tag lists, and the part below the fold is still doing real work even though almost nobody reads it in the grid. Write the opening for a human and the rest for the index.","This preview lays the Pin out at 2:3, the ratio Pinterest designs the entire feed around, and cuts the description where the feed cuts it. Add your own image to see how a 1000 x 1500 crop reads. Nothing you type or upload leaves your browser."],tips:["Fit the idea into 50 characters. That is what shows in the feed, and it has to be the reason somebody opens the Pin rather than a partial sentence.","Use the title as well. It stops at 100 characters, is read as search text, and gives you a second visible line to work with.","Write plain keywords, not tag lists. Pinterest reads the title and the description as search text, so a natural sentence about the subject outperforms a row of hashtags.","Keep hashtags to 0 to 5 and put them last. There is a 20 hashtag cap, but a tag block at the start of a description spends the only characters the feed displays.","Export Pins at 1000 x 1500 at 2:3. Taller Pins are truncated in the grid rather than scaled down, so a very long infographic appears as the top of itself.","Put any text in the top third of the image. That is the part guaranteed to be visible in the grid, and it does more work than the description does."],faqs:[{question:"Is this a real Pinterest screenshot?",answer:"No. It is a neutral HTML approximation: a 2:3 media box matching the 1000 x 1500 standard Pin, a username led identity line, and the description cut where the feed cuts it. There is no Pinterest branding or interface in the card."},{question:"Will the description cut at exactly this point on my screen?",answer:"Approximately. Only the first 50 to 60 characters of the description appear under a Pin in the feed, and the rest opens on the Pin page. The exact figure moves with column width and text size, so write to 50 rather than to 60."},{question:"Where does the image I upload go?",answer:"Nowhere. It becomes a local object URL rendered inside your browser. It is never uploaded to us and never reaches Pinterest until you upload it yourself, and the URL is released when you replace the image or close the tab."},{question:"Can I download the Pin mockup as an image?",answer:"Not yet. Copying the description takes one button, but there is no picture export. A screenshot covers it: Cmd, Shift and 4 on macOS, or the Windows key with Shift and S on Windows."},{question:"How long should a Pin description be?",answer:"Write the first 50 characters for the feed and use the remaining 450 for search. The field holds 500 characters in total, the title holds 100, and Pinterest reads both as search text, so the part below the fold is not wasted even though it is not displayed in the grid."},{question:"Does the description matter more than the image?",answer:"No. On Pinterest the image is the post: a 2:3 Pin at 1000 x 1500 with legible text in the top third does more than any description can. The description exists to explain the Pin to search and to give the tap a reason, in that order."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-post-preview","pinterest-character-counter","pinterest-image-resizer","instagram-post-preview"]},...W,"roas-calculator":{slug:"roas-calculator",family:"roas-plan",platform:null,datePublished:Y,lastUpdated:Y,label:"ROAS calculator",metaTitle:"ROAS Calculator (Free Ad Revenue Projection)",metaDescription:"Free ROAS calculator for Meta, Google, TikTok, LinkedIn and Pinterest. Enter a monthly budget and order value to project conversions and return on ad spend.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"ROAS Calculator",heroLead:"Work out what a month of ad spend should return before you spend it. On all-industry benchmarks, $3,000 on Meta at a $60 average order value projects 214,286 impressions, 3,000 clicks, 264 conversions and 5.28x ROAS.",intro:["ROAS is return on ad spend: the revenue an ad account produced divided by what you paid for it. A campaign that spent $3,000 and drove $15,840 in tracked revenue returned 5.28x. It is the one number every media buyer is asked for in the first ten seconds of a review, and the only one that says whether the account should get more money next month or less.","The problem with the question is that most people ask it before there is any data. This calculator answers it from four things you already know or can decide: which platform you are buying on, how much you are spending per month, what an average order is worth to you, and what a visitor is worth once they land. The first three you type. The fourth comes from platform benchmarks, and you can overwrite any of them.","The chain runs budget to impressions to clicks to conversions to revenue. Your budget divided by CPM times 1,000 gives impressions. Impressions times click-through rate gives clicks. Clicks times landing page conversion rate gives conversions. Conversions times average order value gives revenue, and revenue divided by spend is your ROAS. Every stage is computed from the unrounded stage above it, so the number at the bottom is not a pile of rounding errors.","The default CPM, CTR and CVR are all-industry blends estimated from public advertising benchmark roundups and platform-published averages. They are a planning starting point, not a forecast: a 2% landing page conversion rate instead of the 8.8% Meta blend turns that same $3,000 month from 264 conversions at 5.28x into 60 conversions at 1.20x. If you have your own numbers from a previous month, put them in. The projection is only ever as good as its worst assumption."],tips:["Know your breakeven ROAS before you look at the projected one. If your gross margin is 40%, you need 2.5x just to stand still, and a 3x month is a thin win rather than a good one. A projection that beats a target you never set is not information.","Replace the benchmark conversion rate first. CPM and CTR sit inside a fairly narrow band across accounts, but landing page conversion rate ranges from under 1% to well past 10%, so it is the assumption that decides whether the projection is useful or decorative.","Read cost per conversion as the order value you need to break even. Revenue equals spend exactly when your average order equals your cost per conversion, so a $44 cost per conversion on TikTok means anything under a $44 basket is losing money before you have paid for anything else.","Give a change two weeks before you judge it. Every platform re-learns after a budget move, an audience edit or a new creative, and the first few days of that period are the most expensive and least representative in the whole month.","Check the platform number against your own analytics once a month. Ad platforms attribute generously to themselves, so reported revenue routinely runs ahead of what the store recorded. Pick one source of truth, write it down, and compare every month against the same one.","Treat creative as the biggest lever in the chain. Bidding tweaks move CPM by single-digit percentages; a hook that works can double click-through rate, and a doubled CTR doubles the whole projection below it without you spending another dollar."],faqs:[{question:"What is a good ROAS?",answer:"It depends entirely on your margin, which is why no single number is right. Divide 1 by your gross margin to get the point where you break even: at a 40% margin that is 2.5x, at a 25% margin it is 4x. Ecommerce accounts commonly target 3x to 4x, subscription businesses often accept under 1x on the first order because the second and third are free, and a lead generation account should be judged on cost per qualified lead rather than on ROAS at all."},{question:"How is ROAS calculated?",answer:"ROAS equals revenue attributed to the ads divided by the amount spent on them. $15,840 in revenue on $3,000 of spend is 5.28x. This calculator gets to revenue the long way, because you do not have it yet: budget divided by CPM times 1,000 gives impressions, impressions times CTR gives clicks, clicks times conversion rate gives conversions, and conversions times average order value gives revenue. Change any one assumption and everything below it moves."},{question:"What is the difference between ROAS and ROI?",answer:"ROAS measures revenue against ad spend only. ROI measures profit against total cost, so it also carries your cost of goods, shipping, payment fees, agency retainers and the salary of whoever is running the account. A 3x ROAS can be a healthy ROI on a software product with a 90% margin and a straight loss on a physical product with a 30% margin. ROAS is the campaign scoreboard, ROI is whether the business made money."},{question:"Where do the CPM, CTR and conversion rate benchmarks come from?",answer:"They are estimates blended from public advertising benchmark roundups of the kind published annually by the large agency and tooling vendors, plus averages the platforms publish themselves. They are all-industry blends, so your vertical, offer and creative move every one of them. The Google row is a search and display blend rather than pure search, which is why its click-through rate sits at 4.0% instead of the higher figure a search-only account would see. Every default can be overwritten in the advanced fields."},{question:"Do I get the full media plan?",answer:"Yes, by email. The projection itself and every benchmark on this page are free to read with no address at all. The budget split, the cost per conversion target and the written assumption list unlock when you ask us to send them, and the emailed version is built from exactly the same public estimates and the same formula you can read here. There is no paid tier and no extra data behind the form."},{question:"Is my data stored?",answer:"The projection runs entirely in your browser and nothing leaves the page while you type. We receive something only if you ask for the plan by email, and then it is your address, the figures you entered and which page you were on, so we can send the plan and know which tool produced it. Marketing email is a separate checkbox that starts unticked."}],relatedToolSlugs:["ads-budget-calculator","engagement-rate-calculator","sponsored-post-rate-calculator","best-time-to-post"]},"ads-budget-calculator":{slug:"ads-budget-calculator",family:"roas-plan",platform:null,datePublished:Y,lastUpdated:Y,label:"Ad budget calculator",metaTitle:"Ad Budget Calculator (Free 70/20/10 Split)",metaDescription:"Free ad budget calculator for Meta, Google, TikTok, LinkedIn and Pinterest. Split any monthly budget 70/20/10 into prospecting, retargeting and testing.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"Ad Budget Calculator",heroLead:"Split a monthly ad budget the way media buyers do: 70% prospecting, 20% retargeting, 10% testing. A $3,000 month becomes $2,100, $600 and $300, each with a cost per conversion it has to hit.",intro:["How much should you spend on ads is the wrong first question. Two accounts running the same $3,000 a month can produce completely different results, because one put all of it behind a single audience it had already exhausted and the other split it between finding new people, closing the people it had already reached, and finding out what to try next month.","The split most media buyers converge on is 70/20/10. Seventy percent goes to prospecting, the cold audiences that decide how big the account can get. Twenty percent goes to retargeting the visitors, viewers and cart abandoners you already paid to acquire, which is the cheapest revenue in the account and also the fastest to run dry. Ten percent goes to testing: new creative, new audiences, new offers, budget you have already decided you are willing to lose.","Sizing each bucket takes two numbers. Your monthly budget, and what an average order is worth to you. From there the platform benchmarks fill in the rest: a $3,000 month on Meta at a $14 CPM buys roughly 214,286 impressions and 3,000 clicks, and at the blended 8.8% landing page conversion rate that is about 264 conversions at $11 each. Those figures tell you whether each bucket is big enough to produce enough conversions to learn anything, which is the real constraint on a small budget.","The rule of thumb worth keeping is that any bucket needs roughly 30 to 50 conversions a month before its numbers mean anything, and each ad set needs enough daily budget to clear the platform learning phase rather than sitting in it forever. If the split leaves a bucket under that line, the answer is not a smaller split. It is fewer ad sets, or a bigger budget, or accepting that this month is a test and not a scale-up."],tips:["Give every ad set enough daily budget to leave the learning phase. Most platforms want somewhere near 50 conversions per ad set per week before they optimise properly, so four ad sets on $600 a month is four ad sets that will each stay in learning forever. Run one.","Scale in 20% steps, not doubles. A large budget jump resets the learning phase and buys you a week of expensive, unrepresentative delivery. Raise the winner by about a fifth, wait three or four days, and raise it again if the cost per conversion held.","Cap retargeting by audience size, not by the percentage. Twenty percent is a starting point: if your site had 4,000 visitors last month, $600 against that audience will hit frequency 6 and start annoying people. Spend what the audience can absorb and push the rest into prospecting.","Keep the testing 10% ring-fenced even in a bad month. It is the only budget that produces next quarter, and it is always the first thing cut when this quarter looks thin, which is exactly how an account runs out of working creative.","Judge an ad on cost per conversion, not on click-through rate. A high CTR ad that converts at half the rate of a quieter one is more expensive per customer, and the platform will happily spend your whole prospecting budget on it because clicks are what it was asked to buy.","Set the target cost per conversion from your margin before launch, then write it down. Gross margin times average order value is the most you can pay for a customer and still break even on the first purchase; anything above that has to be justified by repeat business you can actually measure."],faqs:[{question:"How much should I spend on ads per month?",answer:"Enough for each active ad set to produce roughly 30 to 50 conversions a month, otherwise nothing you learn is reliable. Work backwards from cost per conversion: at $11 per conversion on Meta benchmarks, 50 conversions costs about $550 a month for one ad set, so a $1,000 month can support one prospecting ad set and a small retargeting one, and not much else. If your product costs more to sell, the floor rises with it."},{question:"Why split a budget 70/20/10?",answer:"Because the three jobs have different ceilings. Prospecting is the only bucket that can grow, so it gets the majority. Retargeting converts several times better but is capped by how many people you reached last month, so a larger share just raises frequency against the same small audience. Testing is a fixed cost you pay to have something to scale next quarter. The exact numbers are a convention rather than a law: a new account with no audience to retarget often starts nearer 85/5/10, and a brand with heavy organic traffic can justify 60/30/10."},{question:"How do I set a target cost per acquisition?",answer:"Multiply your average order value by your gross margin. A $60 order at a 40% margin leaves $24, so $24 is the most you can pay for a first purchase and still break even on it. Set the working target below that, commonly at 60% to 70% of the breakeven figure, so the account has room to absorb a bad week. If you know your repeat purchase rate well enough to defend it, you can pay above the first-order breakeven, but only if you are measuring the repeats rather than hoping for them."},{question:"When should I increase my budget?",answer:"When an ad set has held its cost per conversion for at least a week at its current spend, and the audience is not already saturated. Check frequency before you raise anything: a prospecting audience over about 2.5 impressions per person per week is telling you it is running out of new people, and more money there buys repetition rather than reach. Raise by roughly 20%, hold for three or four days, and repeat while the cost holds."},{question:"Do I get the full budget plan?",answer:"Yes, by email. The projection and every benchmark on this page are free to read with no address at all. The bucket by bucket split with dollar amounts, the cost per conversion target and the written assumption list unlock when you ask us to send them, and the emailed version uses exactly the same public estimates and the same arithmetic shown here."},{question:"Is my data stored?",answer:"The calculation runs entirely in your browser and nothing leaves the page while you type. We receive something only if you ask for the plan by email, and then it is your address, the figures you entered and which page you were on, purely so we can deliver it. Marketing email is a separate checkbox that starts unticked."}],relatedToolSlugs:["roas-calculator","engagement-rate-calculator","content-calendar-generator","sponsored-post-rate-calculator"]},"ads-character-counter":{slug:"ads-character-counter",family:"ads-character-counter",platform:null,datePublished:p,lastUpdated:p,label:"Ad character counter",metaTitle:"Ad Character Counter: Limits for 5 Ad Platforms",metaDescription:"Free ad copy character counter for Meta, Google Ads, TikTok, LinkedIn and Pinterest. Check headline, primary text and description limits before you upload a campaign.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"Ad Character Counter",heroLead:"Check ad copy against the real field limits on five networks at once: 40 characters for a Meta headline, 30 for a Google Ads headline, 100 for TikTok ad text, 200 for a LinkedIn headline and 100 for a Pinterest title.",intro:["Every ad platform splits your copy into named fields, and every field has its own ceiling. A Meta ad has primary text, a headline and a link description. A Google responsive search ad has up to 15 headlines of 30 characters and 4 descriptions of 90. A LinkedIn single image ad has 3,000 characters of introductory text and a 200 character headline. Paste the same paragraph into all three and two of them reject it.",'The limits fall into two kinds and the difference decides how you write. A hard cap is where the platform refuses the ad: a 31 character Google headline cannot be saved, full stop. A visible cap is where the placement truncates: Meta serves all 3,000 characters of primary text but the feed hides everything past roughly 125 behind "See more", and LinkedIn hides everything past roughly 150. Nothing breaks, the ad just stops being read.',"That gap is where most ad copy quietly fails. LinkedIn has the widest one in this table, 150 visible against a 3,000 hard cap, which means 95% of a maximum-length LinkedIn ad is invisible unless someone taps. Pinterest shows about the first 40 characters of a 100 character title in the grid. Meta cuts Stories and Reels primary text at about 90, earlier than the 125 it cuts the feed at.","This counter runs your text against the fields of whichever platform you pick, one input per field, and flags four states as you type: it fits, it is inside the last 10% of the usable length, it will be truncated in feed, or it is over the hard cap and will be rejected. Nothing is uploaded, nothing is stored, and there is no email step. For organic captions rather than paid copy, use the social media character counter instead."],tips:["Decide which cap you are writing to before you start. If a field has a visible cap, that is the real limit: 125 characters on Meta primary text and 150 on LinkedIn introductory text, not the 3,000 both of them technically accept.","Write the tightest platform first. Cutting a 3,000 character LinkedIn intro down to a 30 character Google headline destroys it; writing the 30 character version first and then expanding almost always survives the trip.","Google is the only network here that never truncates. Search rejects an over-length headline outright, so there is no soft landing: 30 characters means 30, and the ad simply will not save at 31.","Count assets, not just characters. A responsive search ad wants up to 15 headlines and 4 descriptions, with minimums of 3 and 2, and only three headlines and two descriptions ever show at once. Fifteen near-identical headlines is fifteen ways to say the same thing.","Check the placement, not just the platform. Meta cuts primary text at about 125 in feed and about 90 in Stories and Reels, and clips the headline at about 27 in feed against about 25 in full screen. One line of copy is not automatically safe everywhere the campaign runs.","Front-load the offer in every field that truncates. On Meta, LinkedIn and Pinterest the first line is the whole ad for most of the people who see it, and everything after the fold is read only by people who already decided to keep reading."],faqs:[{question:"What is the character limit for a Facebook ad?",answer:'Meta ads have three copy fields. Primary text takes up to 3,000 characters but the feed shows about 125 before a "See more" link hides the rest, and Stories and Reels cut it at about 90. The headline is 40 characters, of which about 27 show in feed. The link description is 30 characters and is not displayed on every placement.'},{question:"What is the Google Ads headline character limit?",answer:"Thirty characters per headline, and a responsive search ad takes up to 15 of them with a minimum of 3. Descriptions are 90 characters each, up to 4 with a minimum of 2, and the two optional display paths are 15 characters each. Google enforces these in the builder: an over-length headline cannot be saved, it is never truncated at serve time."},{question:"Which ad platform gives you the most copy?",answer:"Meta and LinkedIn tie on the hard cap at 3,000 characters of body copy, but that is not the useful comparison. On visible length LinkedIn is the most generous at about 150 characters before the desktop feed collapses the ad, Meta shows about 125 in feed, TikTok caps ad text at 100 characters outright, and Pinterest shows about 40 characters of title in the grid."},{question:"What happens if my ad copy is too long?",answer:'It depends which cap you crossed. Past a hard cap the platform rejects the ad or the field stops accepting input, so you find out immediately. Past a visible cap the ad runs normally and the placement truncates the display, usually behind "See more" or an ellipsis, so you find out only when the copy underperforms and nobody can tell you why.'},{question:"Do emoji count as characters in ad copy?",answer:"They count, and on some networks they are worse than that. TikTok blocks emoji and several special characters in ad text on a number of placements, so plain text is the only thing guaranteed to pass review. Where emoji are allowed they still spend characters, and a decorative emoji at the front of a 40 character Meta headline is a real cost."},{question:"Is this different from a caption character counter?",answer:"Yes, and the numbers are not interchangeable. This tool measures paid fields: headlines, primary text, descriptions and display paths, each with its own cap. Organic posts are one long caption field with hashtag and mention caps on top, so for an Instagram, X or LinkedIn post use the social media character counter instead."}],relatedToolSlugs:["meta-ads-character-counter","google-ads-character-counter","linkedin-ads-character-counter","social-media-character-counter","roas-calculator"]},"meta-ads-character-counter":{slug:"meta-ads-character-counter",family:"ads-character-counter",platform:null,datePublished:p,lastUpdated:p,label:"Meta ads character counter",metaTitle:"Facebook Ad Character Limits: Headline and Primary Text",metaDescription:"Facebook and Instagram ad character limits: 40 character headline, 30 character description, 3,000 character primary text that the feed cuts at about 125. Count yours free.",heroEyebrow:"Free Meta ads tool",heroTitle:"Facebook and Instagram Ad Character Counter",heroLead:"A Meta ad has three copy fields: 3,000 characters of primary text that the feed cuts at about 125, a 40 character headline that clips at about 27, and a 30 character link description.",intro:["Meta ad copy is written into three fields in Ads Manager and each one behaves differently once the ad is live. Primary text is the paragraph above the creative. The headline is the bold line under it. The link description is a short supporting line that some placements show and many do not.",'Only one of those numbers is a wall. Primary text inherits the organic post ceiling, which is why 3,000 is recorded here as a conservative working cap rather than a documented rejection point, and you will never reach it anyway: the feed collapses primary text behind "See more" at roughly 125 characters. Stories and Reels cut it earlier still, at about 90, because the text overlays the creative and competes with the profile row and the call to action button.',"The headline is 40 characters and about 27 of them survive on a narrow phone in feed, dropping to about 25 in Stories and Reels. That is the field most people overwrite: a 40 character headline that reads perfectly in the Ads Manager preview loses its last three words on the device it actually ships to. The 30 character link description is optional support for the headline, never the place the offer lives.","This counter checks all of it per placement, so a line that fits the feed but breaks in Reels shows up before the campaign does. It flags four states: fits, close to the limit, will be truncated, and over the hard cap. For organic Facebook or Instagram captions, which are one long field with their own hashtag and mention caps, use the social media character counter instead."],tips:['Write primary text to 125 characters, not to 3,000. That is roughly one strong sentence, and it is everything the feed shows before "See more" swallows the rest.',"Write a second, shorter opening if the campaign runs in Stories and Reels. Those placements cut primary text at about 90 characters, 35 fewer than the feed, and full screen placements are where the fold hurts most.","Keep the headline inside about 27 characters even though Meta accepts 40. The last 13 characters are the ones a narrow phone drops, so they can carry emphasis but never meaning.","Do not put the offer in the 30 character link description. It is not shown on every placement, so anything that only exists there is invisible to part of your audience.","Count the emoji. A flag or a skin tone variant can be several code points, and a decorative emoji at the front of a 40 character headline costs the same as a short word that could have been doing work.","Check the copy against the placement mix in the ad set, not against the preview you happen to be looking at. Advantage+ placements put the same ad into feed, Stories and Reels, each with a different cut."],faqs:[{question:"What is the character limit for Facebook ad primary text?",answer:'Primary text takes up to 3,000 characters, but that ceiling is inherited from the organic post field rather than published as an ad rejection point. The number that matters is about 125 characters, which is where the feed truncates and drops in a "See more" link. Stories and Reels cut earlier, at about 90.'},{question:"How many characters is a Facebook ad headline?",answer:"Forty characters. Around 27 of them show on a narrow phone in feed before the headline wraps or clips, and full screen placements clip harder still at around 25. Meta warns at 40 in the builder, so treat it as a hard cap and 27 as the working one."},{question:"Are Instagram ad limits different from Facebook ad limits?",answer:"No. Instagram and Facebook ads are built in the same Ads Manager fields with the same caps: 3,000 characters of primary text, a 40 character headline and a 30 character link description. What differs is the placement, and Instagram campaigns lean much harder on Stories and Reels, where primary text cuts at about 90 rather than 125."},{question:"What is the Facebook ad link description limit?",answer:"Thirty characters. It sits under the headline as supporting text and it is not rendered on every placement, so treat it as optional reinforcement. Anything essential belongs in the headline or in the first line of primary text."},{question:"Does Meta reject ads that are too long?",answer:"For the headline and description, effectively yes: the builder warns at 40 and 30 and most placements cut well before those points. For primary text Meta truncates rather than rejects, so a 900 character paragraph runs perfectly happily and is simply hidden past about 125 characters in feed."},{question:"Is there still a 20 percent text rule on ad images?",answer:"No. Meta retired the hard 20 percent text-in-image restriction, so an image heavy with text is no longer rejected outright. Text density can still affect delivery and cost, and none of that touches the copy fields measured here, which are counted independently of anything on the creative."}],relatedToolSlugs:["ads-character-counter","google-ads-character-counter","social-media-character-counter","social-media-post-preview","roas-calculator"]},"google-ads-character-counter":{slug:"google-ads-character-counter",family:"ads-character-counter",platform:null,datePublished:p,lastUpdated:p,label:"Google Ads character counter",metaTitle:"Google Ads Character Limits: RSA Headlines and Descriptions",metaDescription:"Google Ads character limits: 15 headlines at 30 characters, 4 descriptions at 90, 2 display paths at 15. Count responsive search ad and Performance Max copy free.",heroEyebrow:"Free Google Ads tool",heroTitle:"Google Ads Character Counter",heroLead:"A responsive search ad takes up to 15 headlines of 30 characters, 4 descriptions of 90, and 2 display paths of 15. Google rejects anything longer rather than truncating it.",intro:["Google Ads is the strictest set of limits in paid media and the easiest to plan around, because nothing here truncates. A responsive search ad headline is 30 characters. A description is 90. A display path is 15. Type a 31st character into a headline and the field stops accepting it, so an over-length asset never reaches an auction in the first place.","The counting problem on Google is not length, it is quantity. A responsive search ad takes up to 15 headlines with a minimum of 3, and up to 4 descriptions with a minimum of 2. Google assembles them at serve time and shows at most three headlines and two descriptions in any single impression, with the second description often dropped on mobile. Every headline therefore has to read correctly on its own and next to any other two, which rules out writing a sentence across three assets.","Performance Max reuses the same 30 character headline and 90 character description caps, then adds fields a search ad does not have: up to 5 long headlines at 90 characters for larger display slots, up to 5 descriptions where the first one is capped at 60 rather than 90, and a single 25 character business name. The 60 character first description is the detail that catches people out, because the field accepts 90 in other positions.","This counter runs one box per field, counts against the hard cap and shows how many assets of that type the format takes, so 15 headlines is one input and a reminder rather than 15 empty boxes. For organic post captions, which work nothing like this, use the social media character counter instead."],tips:["Treat 30 as absolute. Google will not shorten a headline for you, it will refuse to save it, and there is no visible cap to trade against because search never truncates an approved asset.","Write at least 8 to 10 genuinely different headlines rather than 15 near-duplicates. Only three ever show together, so variety is what the asset combination step actually has to work with.",'Never write a sentence across assets. Headlines are shuffled, so "Free shipping on" and "every order over 50" can appear separately, in either order, or not at all.',"Cap the first Performance Max description at 60 characters, not 90. The field accepts more in later positions, which makes the tighter first slot easy to miss until the asset is rejected.","Keep display paths readable rather than accurate. The two 15 character path fields are cosmetic, they do not have to exist on your site, and they are the cheapest relevance signal in the whole ad.","Front-load the keyword and the number. Search results truncate around the ad in ways you do not control, and a headline that opens with the offer survives every layout Google renders."],faqs:[{question:"What is the Google Ads headline character limit?",answer:"Thirty characters per headline, including spaces. A responsive search ad takes up to 15 headlines with a minimum of 3, and Performance Max asset groups use the same 30 character cap with the same 15 asset maximum."},{question:"How many characters is a Google Ads description?",answer:"Ninety characters, with up to 4 descriptions per responsive search ad and a minimum of 2. At most two are shown, and the second is frequently dropped on mobile, so the first description has to work alone. Performance Max allows up to 5 descriptions but caps the first one at 60 characters."},{question:"How many headlines does a responsive search ad need?",answer:"Three at minimum and 15 at maximum. Google shows up to three at a time and mixes them per auction, so the practical target is 8 to 10 distinct angles: different benefits, different proof, different calls to action, not the same claim reworded."},{question:"What is the display path limit in Google Ads?",answer:"Two optional fields of 15 characters each, appended to the display URL. They are cosmetic: the paths do not need to correspond to a real page on your site, and they exist to make the ad look more relevant to the query."},{question:"Does Google truncate ads that are too long?",answer:"No. Google is the only network in this set that never truncates copy, because it never accepts over-length copy in the first place. The field blocks input past the cap and the ad cannot be saved, so the failure happens in the builder rather than in the auction."},{question:"Are Performance Max limits the same as search?",answer:"Mostly, with additions. Headlines are still 30 characters and descriptions still 90, but Performance Max adds up to 5 long headlines at 90 characters for larger display placements, allows 5 descriptions instead of 4 with the first capped at 60, and requires a business name of up to 25 characters. Long headlines can be truncated to fit a slot, unlike search assets."}],relatedToolSlugs:["ads-character-counter","meta-ads-character-counter","roas-calculator","ads-budget-calculator","social-media-character-counter"]},"tiktok-ads-character-counter":{slug:"tiktok-ads-character-counter",family:"ads-character-counter",platform:null,datePublished:p,lastUpdated:p,label:"TikTok ads character counter",metaTitle:"TikTok Ads Character Limit: 100 Characters of Ad Text",metaDescription:"TikTok in-feed ad text is 1 to 100 characters and the display name is 40. Spark ads use the 2,200 character organic caption. Count your TikTok ad copy free.",heroEyebrow:"Free TikTok ads tool",heroTitle:"TikTok Ads Character Counter",heroLead:"TikTok in-feed ad text runs from 1 to 100 characters, the display name takes 40, and a Spark ad inherits the 2,200 character caption of the organic post it promotes.",intro:["TikTok has the shortest body copy field in paid social. An in-feed ad gets 100 characters of ad text and that is the whole of it: no headline, no description, no second line. The minimum is real too, at one character, because a blank ad text is rejected rather than defaulted to something.","A hundred characters is roughly one sentence, which matches how the copy is actually consumed. The caption sits over the video, is cut after roughly the first line in feed, and expands on tap. TikTok does not publish a character figure for that cut, so this tool records no visible cap for ad text rather than inventing one, and counts against the 100 character hard cap instead.","Non-Spark ads also carry a display name of up to 40 characters, which is the advertiser name set on the ad rather than taken from the account. Spark ads work differently: they promote an existing organic post, so the copy is the organic caption at up to 2,200 characters and it cannot be edited from the ad. If the caption is wrong, fix it on the original post before boosting, not after.","One more constraint that is not a length: emoji and several special characters are blocked in ad text on a number of TikTok placements. Plain text always passes review, which is why this counter measures what you typed rather than assuming decorative characters will survive. For organic TikTok captions, which run to 4,000 characters, use the social media character counter instead."],tips:["Write to 100 characters as one complete sentence. There is no second field to continue into and no headline to carry the offer, so the ad text has to say the whole thing.","Put the payoff in the first few words. The caption is cut after roughly the first line in feed and only expands on tap, and TikTok publishes no figure for where that cut lands, so the safe assumption is early.","Use plain text. Emoji and some special characters are blocked in ad text on several placements, and an ad rejected on review costs more time than the emoji was ever going to earn.","Set the display name deliberately on non-Spark ads. Forty characters is generous, but it is the name people read next to the video and it is not automatically your account name.","Fix the caption before you Spark it. A Spark ad runs the organic post as it stands, caption included, and the 2,200 character organic limit applies rather than the 100 character ad text field.","Do not paste a Meta primary text block in here. A 125 character Meta opener is already 25 characters over what TikTok will accept, which is exactly the kind of silent failure a cross-platform launch produces."],faqs:[{question:"What is the TikTok ad text character limit?",answer:"One hundred characters for an in-feed ad, with a minimum of one. TikTok rejects an empty ad text rather than substituting anything, so the field is genuinely required, and 100 is a hard cap rather than a truncation point."},{question:"Does TikTok truncate ad captions?",answer:"The in-feed caption is cut after roughly the first line on most devices and expands when someone taps it. TikTok does not publish a character figure for that cut, so this tool records no visible cap and counts against the 100 character hard limit instead of guessing a number."},{question:"Can I use emoji in TikTok ad text?",answer:"Not reliably. Emoji and several special characters are blocked in ad text on a number of placements, and the failure shows up at review rather than while you type. Plain text always passes, which makes it the safe default for anything you need live on a schedule."},{question:"What is the character limit for a TikTok Spark ad?",answer:"A Spark ad promotes an existing organic post, so the caption limit is the organic one at up to 2,200 characters and it cannot be edited from the ad. The 100 character ad text field does not apply, because there is no separate ad text to write."},{question:"What is the TikTok ad display name limit?",answer:"Forty characters. It applies to non-Spark ads, where the advertiser name is set on the ad rather than inherited from the account, and it is the name shown next to the video while it plays."},{question:"Is TikTok ad text the same as a TikTok caption?",answer:"No, and the gap is large. An organic TikTok video description runs to 4,000 characters, while in-feed ad text stops at 100. If you are writing an organic post rather than a paid one, use the social media character counter, which measures the 4,000 character field and the hashtags inside it."}],relatedToolSlugs:["ads-character-counter","meta-ads-character-counter","tiktok-character-counter","social-media-character-counter","roas-calculator"]},"linkedin-ads-character-counter":{slug:"linkedin-ads-character-counter",family:"ads-character-counter",platform:null,datePublished:p,lastUpdated:p,label:"LinkedIn ads character counter",metaTitle:"LinkedIn Ads Character Limits: Intro Text and Headline",metaDescription:"LinkedIn ad limits: 3,000 characters of introductory text that truncates at about 150, a 200 character headline that cuts at 70, and 25 by 75 text ads. Count yours free.",heroEyebrow:"Free LinkedIn ads tool",heroTitle:"LinkedIn Ads Character Counter",heroLead:"A LinkedIn single image ad accepts 3,000 characters of introductory text but shows about 150, and accepts a 200 character headline but shows about 70. Text ads are far tighter at 25 and 75.",intro:['LinkedIn has the widest gap between what an ad accepts and what an ad displays of any network in this set. Introductory text on a single image ad has a hard cap of 3,000 characters and a visible cap of roughly 150 on the desktop feed. Write to the ceiling and 95% of your copy sits behind a "see more" link that most of the feed never clicks.',"The headline behaves the same way at a smaller scale: 200 characters accepted, around 70 shown before it is cut on desktop. Neither of these is a rejection, which is what makes them dangerous. The campaign launches, the ad serves, the impressions arrive, and the argument you spent an afternoon on is invisible unless someone expands it.","Text ads are a completely different format with completely different numbers. The headline is 25 characters and the description is 75, both hard caps with no truncation because the sidebar slot holds exactly that much and no more. A 25 character headline is about four words, which is why text ads live or die on the offer rather than on the writing.",'This counter treats the visible cap as the binding one wherever a field has both, so a 400 character intro reads as "will be truncated" rather than as a pass. For organic LinkedIn posts, which are one 3,000 character field that folds at about 210 on desktop, use the social media character counter instead.'],tips:['Write the first 150 characters as if they were the whole ad. That is what the desktop feed shows before "see more", and it is the only part you can count on being read.',"Do not treat 3,000 characters as an invitation. The extra room is real and occasionally useful for context or compliance language, but every character past 150 is opt-in reading rather than delivered copy.","Keep the headline inside 70 characters even though LinkedIn accepts 200. The 130 characters past the cut are served, paid for, and never seen on desktop.","Ask a question or state a number in the first line. LinkedIn ads that earn the expand tend to open with something specific enough to be worth resolving, and generic openers waste the only 150 characters you control.","Budget separately for text ads. A 25 character headline and a 75 character description cannot be trimmed down from single image ad copy, they have to be written for the slot.","Remember that mobile shows less than desktop. The 150 and 70 figures are the desktop cuts, so on a mobile-heavy campaign the safe opener is shorter still."],faqs:[{question:"What is the LinkedIn ad character limit?",answer:"A single image ad takes up to 3,000 characters of introductory text and a 200 character headline. Both truncate long before those points: about 150 characters of intro text and about 70 of the headline are visible on the desktop feed before the rest is collapsed."},{question:"Where does LinkedIn truncate ad intro text?",answer:'At roughly 150 characters on desktop, behind a "see more" link. LinkedIn truncates rather than rejects, so the ad runs exactly as submitted and only the display is cut. The gap between 150 visible and 3,000 accepted is the widest of any platform in this comparison.'},{question:"How many characters is a LinkedIn ad headline?",answer:"Two hundred characters on a single image ad, of which around 70 show before the headline is cut on desktop. Text ads are a separate format with a much tighter 25 character headline that is a hard cap with no truncation at all."},{question:"What are the LinkedIn text ad limits?",answer:"Twenty-five characters for the headline and 75 for the description. Both are hard caps: the sidebar slot holds that much and the builder will not accept more, so there is no visible-versus-accepted trade-off to manage."},{question:"Are sponsored content limits the same as organic post limits?",answer:"The hard cap is the same 3,000 characters, but the fold is not. An organic LinkedIn post collapses at about 210 characters on desktop, while a sponsored single image ad collapses at about 150, because the ad frame gives up room to the headline and the call to action. Use the social media character counter for organic posts."},{question:"Does LinkedIn count hashtags against the ad limit?",answer:"Yes, hashtags are just text and spend the same characters as words. In paid placements they earn far less than they do organically, so on an ad with only 150 visible characters a hashtag block is usually the first thing worth cutting."}],relatedToolSlugs:["ads-character-counter","meta-ads-character-counter","linkedin-character-counter","social-media-character-counter","ads-budget-calculator"]},"pinterest-ads-character-counter":{slug:"pinterest-ads-character-counter",family:"ads-character-counter",platform:null,datePublished:p,lastUpdated:p,label:"Pinterest ads character counter",metaTitle:"Pinterest Ads Character Limits: Title and Description",metaDescription:"Pinterest ad limits: a 100 character title that the grid cuts at about 40, and a 500 character description that shows about 50. Count Promoted Pin copy free.",heroEyebrow:"Free Pinterest ads tool",heroTitle:"Pinterest Ads Character Counter",heroLead:"A Promoted Pin takes a 100 character title and a 500 character description, but the grid shows roughly the first 40 characters of the title and about 50 of the description once a Pin is opened.",intro:["Pinterest ads have two copy fields and both of them are read twice: once by a person scrolling a grid, and once by the search index that decides which grids the Pin appears in. The title has a hard cap of 100 characters and the description a hard cap of 500, and neither number is what a viewer sees.","In the grid, roughly the first 40 characters of the title show before it is cut. The description is not displayed in the feed at all on most surfaces, and once someone opens the Pin only about the first 50 to 60 characters are shown before the rest is collapsed. So the visible budget for a Promoted Pin is around 40 characters of title and around 50 of description, against hard caps of 100 and 500.","That is a case for writing the full 500 anyway, which is unusual advice on a page about character limits. The whole description feeds Pinterest search even though almost nobody reads past the first line, so the characters past the fold are working as metadata rather than as copy. Front-load the payoff for the human, then keep writing for the index.",'Idea ads use the same numbers: a 100 character title cut at about 40, and a 500 character description shown on the detail view rather than the grid at about 50. This counter treats the visible cap as the binding one, so a 90 character title reads as "will be truncated" rather than as a pass. For organic Pins, use the social media character counter instead.'],tips:["Put the subject in the first 40 characters of the title. That is the whole visible title in the grid, and a title that gets cut mid-phrase reads as broken rather than as intriguing.","Make the first 50 characters of the description a complete thought. That is roughly all that shows once the Pin is opened, so a fragment that depends on the next clause is wasted.","Write the remaining 450 characters anyway. They are indexed for Pinterest search even though they are hidden, which makes the description the only field in this whole family where writing past the fold reliably pays.","Use the words a person would type into Pinterest search, not campaign language. Pinterest behaves like a search engine wearing a feed, and its matching reads the title, the description and the board context together.","Give every Pin variant its own title and description. Duplicating copy across variants pointing at the same URL wastes the second Pin, because it competes with the first for the same queries.","Idea ads follow the same caps as Promoted Pins, so one set of copy covers both. The difference is where the description appears, on the detail view rather than the grid, not how long it can be."],faqs:[{question:"What is the Pinterest ad title character limit?",answer:"One hundred characters is the hard cap for a Promoted Pin or Idea ad title. Roughly the first 40 characters show in the grid before Pinterest cuts the title, so 40 is the number to write to and 100 is the number the field accepts."},{question:"How long can a Pinterest ad description be?",answer:"Five hundred characters. It is hidden in the feed on most surfaces and only partly shown once someone opens the Pin, at roughly 50 to 60 characters, but the full text still feeds Pinterest search, so the hidden part is doing real work."},{question:"Should I write the full 500 character description?",answer:"Yes, and Pinterest is the only platform in this family where that is true. Everywhere else the copy past the fold is simply unread. On Pinterest it is indexed, so the first 50 characters sell the click and the remaining 450 help the Pin get found in the first place."},{question:"Does Pinterest reject ads that are too long?",answer:"Past 100 characters on the title or 500 on the description, yes: those are hard caps and the field will not take more. Below them Pinterest truncates rather than rejects, so a 90 character title runs perfectly well and is simply cut at about 40 in the grid."},{question:"Are Idea ad limits different from Promoted Pin limits?",answer:"No. Both take a 100 character title cut at about 40 in the grid and a 500 character description shown at about 50. The only difference is where the description surfaces: on an Idea ad it appears on the detail view rather than under the Pin in the feed."},{question:"Do hashtags help in Pinterest ad descriptions?",answer:"Far less than plain descriptive language does. Pinterest has leaned back toward keyword text, so a description written in the words someone would actually search outperforms a tag list, and every hashtag spends characters from the same 500."}],relatedToolSlugs:["ads-character-counter","meta-ads-character-counter","pinterest-character-counter","social-media-character-counter","roas-calculator"]},"cpm-calculator":{slug:"cpm-calculator",family:"ad-metrics",platform:null,datePublished:m,lastUpdated:m,label:"CPM calculator",metaTitle:"CPM Calculator: Cost Per 1,000 Impressions",metaDescription:"Free CPM calculator. Enter spend and impressions to price a thousand impressions, or fill any two of the three fields and read the third. No signup.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"CPM Calculator",heroLead:"Work out what a thousand impressions costs you. Spend $2,400 for 300,000 impressions and your CPM is $8.00. Fill any two of spend, impressions and CPM, and the third one fills itself in.",intro:["CPM is the price of a thousand impressions. Divide what you spent by the impressions it bought, multiply by a thousand because the price is quoted per thousand rather than per one, and that is the whole formula: $2,400 across 300,000 impressions is an $8.00 CPM. It is the rawest number in an ad account, the price of attention before anybody has clicked anything.","Half of that price is the market and half of it is you. Some audiences are simply scarcer than others, which is why a thousand impressions in front of LinkedIn professionals runs near $33 while a thousand on Pinterest runs near $6 on all-industry blends. The rest moves with decisions you control: how narrow the targeting is, which placements you allowed, what season you are bidding in, and how much the auction likes the creative it is being asked to deliver.","This calculator links all three numbers rather than just one of them. Enter spend and impressions to price a campaign that already ran. Enter a CPM and a budget to see how much reach next month actually buys. Enter a CPM and an impression target to find the budget that reaches it. Same formula every time, solved for whichever box you left alone, updating as you type.","The trap is treating a lower CPM as a win by itself. Nobody sells impressions. A $6 CPM in front of people who will never buy is more expensive than a $33 CPM in front of people who will, so the benchmark table below is a sanity check on a bid rather than a scoreboard to climb. What CPM is genuinely good for is diagnosis: when it moves 40% in a week and you changed nothing, something in the auction, the audience or the delivery changed on your behalf."],tips:["Compare CPM inside one platform and one audience, never across two. It is the price of a specific set of people at a specific moment, so a $14 Meta impression and a $38 Google impression are different products, not the same product at two prices.","Check frequency before you blame the auction. Once a prospecting audience has largely been covered, the platform has to work harder to find someone new and charges you for it, and the fix is a wider audience rather than a lower bid.","Expect the fourth quarter to cost more. Retail bidding from October into late December raises the price of the same impression for everyone, so compare a November CPM against last November rather than against October.","Ignore the first three days after any change. A budget move, an audience edit or a fresh creative restarts the learning phase, and those are the most expensive and least representative impressions the ad set will ever buy.","Use CPM to price reach and nothing else. If a launch needs 300,000 people to see it and your CPM is $8.00, the budget is $2,400 and the conversation is finished. That is the one question it answers exactly.","Look at placement mix when CPM moves and you did not touch anything. Feed impressions cost several times what an audience network or a sidebar impression does, so delivery drifting between placements moves a blended CPM without a single auction behaving differently."],faqs:[{question:"How do you calculate CPM?",answer:"CPM = spend / impressions x 1,000. Divide the money spent by the number of impressions it bought, then multiply by a thousand because CPM is quoted per thousand impressions rather than per one. A campaign that spent $2,400 and served 300,000 impressions has a CPM of $8.00. The same formula run backwards prices reach: at an $8.00 CPM, 300,000 impressions costs $2,400, and $3,000 buys 375,000."},{question:"What is a good CPM?",answer:"It depends almost entirely on who you are buying, which is why a single good number does not exist. On all-industry blended estimates, Pinterest sits near $6 and TikTok near $10, Meta near $14, and the two expensive audiences are LinkedIn near $33 and Google near $38. Those are planning anchors rather than targets: a niche B2B audience on Meta can cost more per thousand than a broad consumer audience on LinkedIn, and both can be the right buy."},{question:"Why did my CPM suddenly go up?",answer:"Five causes cover almost every case. The audience is saturated and the platform is reaching further to find someone new. It is a high-demand season and every advertiser is bidding against you. You changed something and the ad set is relearning. Delivery shifted toward more expensive placements. Or the creative has fatigued, and the auction quietly charges more to deliver something people have stopped responding to. Check frequency first, because it is the one you can act on the same day."},{question:"Is a lower CPM always better?",answer:"No, and chasing it is how accounts end up with cheap impressions and no revenue. CPM only measures what attention cost, never what it was worth. Broad targeting, cheap placements and low-quality inventory all reliably lower CPM while making everything downstream worse. Treat a falling CPM as good news only when the cost per customer fell with it."},{question:"Are impressions the same as reach?",answer:"No, and mixing them up will make your CPM look wrong. An impression is one delivery of the ad, so one person who sees it four times is four impressions and one reach. CPM is always priced on impressions, so if you divide spend by reach instead you get cost per person, which will be a much larger number and is not comparable to any published CPM benchmark."},{question:"Is this calculator free, and is anything stored?",answer:"It is free, there is no email step, and it runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you type is sent anywhere, and the benchmark table and worked examples below are printed in full on the page, so you can read every number without entering anything at all."}],relatedToolSlugs:["cpc-calculator","ctr-calculator","cpa-calculator","roas-calculator","ads-budget-calculator"]},"cpc-calculator":{slug:"cpc-calculator",family:"ad-metrics",platform:null,datePublished:m,lastUpdated:m,label:"CPC calculator",metaTitle:"CPC Calculator (Free Cost Per Click Tool)",metaDescription:"Free CPC calculator. Enter spend and clicks to get your cost per click, or fill any two of spend, clicks and CPC to solve the third. Benchmarks included.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"CPC Calculator",heroLead:"Work out what one click costs you. Spend $1,800 for 1,500 clicks and your CPC is $1.20. Fill any two of spend, clicks and CPC, and the calculator solves the one you left blank.",intro:["Cost per click is spend divided by clicks. Nothing more complicated than that: $1,800 across 1,500 clicks is a $1.20 CPC. It is the price of one person choosing to leave a feed and arrive on your site, which makes it the last number in the account that belongs entirely to the ads and the first one that starts to feel like traffic.","CPC is worth watching precisely because it is a compound number. You never bid on it directly on most platforms, you pay for delivery and receive clicks, so a CPC is really the cost of the impressions divided by how many of them earned a click. That is why two accounts paying the same for reach can pay wildly different amounts per visitor, and why a creative change can halve a CPC without a single bid being touched.","The calculator links the three figures so you can fill any two and read the third. Spend and clicks give you the CPC of a campaign that already ran. A CPC and a budget tell you how much traffic next month buys. A CPC and a click target tell you the budget that traffic needs. Everything recalculates as you type, and editing any field hands the calculation to the other two.","What CPC cannot tell you is whether the click was worth having. On blended benchmarks a Pinterest click and a Meta click both land near $1.00 while a LinkedIn click sits near $5.50, and the LinkedIn click is often the cheaper one once you count what happens after the landing page. Cheap traffic that never converts is the most expensive thing an ad account can buy, so read CPC as the price of a visit rather than as a verdict on the campaign."],tips:["Judge CPC against the value of a visit, not against another account. A $5.50 click that closes a $900 contract is a bargain and a $0.30 click that bounces is not, which is why blended benchmarks belong in the planning stage rather than in the weekly review.","Improve the click-through rate before you touch the bid. You are buying delivery and receiving clicks, so more clicks out of the same impressions is a direct discount on every visit, and creative moves that ratio far harder than bidding does.","Check the landing page load time when CPC looks fine but sessions do not. Platforms count a click when the ad is tapped, analytics counts a session when the page loads, and a slow page can lose a fifth of them in the gap. You paid for both.","Split CPC by placement before concluding a platform got expensive. One expensive placement quietly taking a bigger share of delivery raises a blended CPC while every individual auction stays exactly where it was.","Watch CPC and conversion rate together, always. A cheaper click that converts at half the rate has doubled the cost of a customer, and the account that optimises for cheap clicks will find that trade every single time.","Expect CPC to drift up as an audience saturates. Frequency rises, response falls, and the same spend buys fewer clicks. That is an audience problem rather than a bidding one, and a bid cut usually just buys less of the same."],faqs:[{question:"How do you calculate CPC?",answer:"CPC = spend / clicks. Divide the amount spent by the number of clicks it produced: $1,800 spent for 1,500 clicks is a $1.20 cost per click. Run it backwards to plan instead of report: at $1.20 a click, a $600 budget buys 500 clicks, and 2,000 clicks needs $2,400."},{question:"What is a good CPC?",answer:"Anything below what a visit is worth to you. As a planning anchor, all-industry blended estimates put a Meta or Pinterest click near $1.00, a Google click near $0.95, a TikTok click near $1.11 and a LinkedIn click near $5.50. Those come from dividing each platform benchmark CPM by the clicks its benchmark click-through rate produces, so treat them as the shape of the market rather than as a target to beat."},{question:"Why is my CPC so high?",answer:"Usually because too few of the impressions you are paying for turn into clicks. Either the audience is wrong for the offer, the creative has stopped earning attention, or the audience has seen it too often. The other common cause is the buy itself: expensive placements, a narrow audience or a competitive season all raise the price of the impressions that CPC is calculated from. Fix the click rate first, because it is the lever that moves without spending more."},{question:"What is the difference between CPC and CPM?",answer:"CPM prices a thousand impressions, CPC prices one click, and on most platforms you buy the first and receive the second. They can move in opposite directions in the same week: impressions getting more expensive while a stronger creative earns enough extra clicks that each visit still costs less. That is why an account should watch both, and never conclude anything from one of them alone."},{question:"Does a cheap click mean a cheap customer?",answer:"No, and assuming it does is the most expensive mistake in paid media. What happens after the click decides the cost of a customer: two campaigns at the same $1.20 CPC produce customers at $24 and at $120 if one landing page converts at 5% and the other at 1%. Cheap traffic that does not convert is not cheap, it is just quiet."},{question:"Is this calculator free, and is anything stored?",answer:"It is free, ungated and runs entirely in your browser. There is no email step and nothing you type leaves the page. The derived per-platform figures and the worked examples are all printed below, so the page is fully readable without entering anything."}],relatedToolSlugs:["ctr-calculator","cpm-calculator","cpa-calculator","roas-calculator","ads-budget-calculator"]},"cpa-calculator":{slug:"cpa-calculator",family:"ad-metrics",platform:null,datePublished:m,lastUpdated:m,label:"CPA calculator",metaTitle:"CPA Calculator: Cost Per Acquisition",metaDescription:"Free CPA calculator. Enter spend and conversions to get cost per acquisition, or fill any two of spend, conversions and CPA to solve the third.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"CPA Calculator",heroLead:"Work out what one customer costs you. Spend $3,000 for 264 conversions and your CPA is $11.36. Fill any two of spend, conversions and CPA, and the third is calculated for you.",intro:["Cost per acquisition is spend divided by conversions: $3,000 across 264 conversions is a CPA of $11.36. It is the number that decides whether an ad account is a business or a hobby, because unlike everything above it in the funnel it is denominated in the same units as your margin. You can compare a CPA directly to what a customer is worth and know immediately which way the trade went.","The word acquisition is doing a lot of work, so define it before you use the number. A conversion can be a purchase, a trial start, a booked demo or a form fill, and each definition produces a completely different CPA from identical spend. A $40 cost per lead and a $40 cost per paying customer are not the same result and should never sit in the same column of a report.","The calculator links spend, conversions and CPA so you can fill any two and read the third. Spend and conversions price a month that already happened. A CPA and a budget tell you how many customers next month buys. A CPA and a customer target tell you what that target costs. Editing any field hands the calculation to the other two, so it works as a planning tool as easily as a reporting one.","CPA only becomes a decision once you have a ceiling to compare it to, and that ceiling comes from margin rather than from a benchmark. Gross margin times average order value is the most you can pay for a first purchase and still break even: a $60 order at a 40% margin leaves $24, so $24 is the line and a $11.36 CPA is comfortably under it. Without that line, a CPA is just a number that went up or down."],tips:["Write down what counts as an acquisition before you compare anything. Purchases, trials, leads and demos all produce a CPA, and a report that mixes two of them is arithmetic on top of a category error.","Set the ceiling from margin, not from a benchmark. Average order value times gross margin is the most a first purchase can cost you, and a working target at roughly 60% to 70% of that leaves the account room to absorb a bad week.","Watch CPA against click cost and conversion rate together. CPA rising while clicks got cheaper means the landing page or the offer moved, and no amount of bid tuning will fix it.","Give each ad set enough conversions to be readable. Somewhere near 30 to 50 a month is the point where a CPA stops being noise, and below that you are reading random variation as performance.","Count repeat business only if you actually measure it. Paying above the first-order ceiling is defensible when you know the repeat rate and can prove it, and is how accounts quietly go broke when the repeat rate is a hope.","Compare platform CPA to platform order value, never on its own. A $137.50 cost per acquisition is ruinous against a $60 basket and healthy against a $900 contract, which is what the same audience being expensive and valuable looks like in one number."],faqs:[{question:"How do you calculate CPA?",answer:"CPA = spend / conversions. Divide the amount spent by the number of conversions it produced: $3,000 for 264 conversions is a CPA of $11.36. Run it the other way to plan: at that CPA, 500 conversions needs about $5,682, and a $1,000 budget projects about 88."},{question:"What is a good CPA?",answer:"Any figure below your margin per customer, which makes it specific to you rather than to your industry. Work out the ceiling first: average order value times gross margin is the most a first purchase can cost. For a sense of scale, chaining the platform benchmarks together puts an expected cost per acquisition near $11.36 on Meta, $19.00 on Google, $44.44 on TikTok, $50.00 on Pinterest and $137.50 on LinkedIn, which mostly reflects who each platform is selling you rather than how well each one performs."},{question:"What is the difference between CPA, CPL and CAC?",answer:"CPA is the cost of whichever conversion you defined. CPL narrows that to a lead, so it is a CPA on a cheaper event further from revenue. CAC is the whole cost of acquiring a customer, so it carries every channel plus the salaries, tools and discounts that ads never see. An account can hit its CPA target every month and still have a CAC the business cannot afford."},{question:"Why did my CPA rise while my click costs fell?",answer:"Because the two are separated by everything that happens after the click. Cheaper traffic that converts at a lower rate raises the cost per customer even as the cost per visit falls, and that is exactly what broadening an audience or moving to cheaper placements tends to produce. Look at conversion rate and landing page behaviour before you touch the campaign."},{question:"How do I set a target CPA?",answer:"Start from the ceiling, then work below it. Multiply your average order value by gross margin to find the most you can pay and still break even on the first order: $60 at a 40% margin is $24. Set the working target at roughly 60% to 70% of that, so a bad week does not immediately put the account underwater, and only raise it above the ceiling if you can measure repeat purchases rather than assume them."},{question:"Is this calculator free, and is anything stored?",answer:"Free, ungated and entirely local to your browser. There is no email step, nothing you type is transmitted, and every derived figure and worked example on the page is server rendered, so the numbers are readable with JavaScript switched off."}],relatedToolSlugs:["break-even-roas-calculator","cpc-calculator","cpm-calculator","roas-calculator","ads-budget-calculator"]},"ctr-calculator":{slug:"ctr-calculator",family:"ad-metrics",platform:null,datePublished:m,lastUpdated:m,label:"CTR calculator",metaTitle:"CTR Calculator: Click-Through Rate (Free)",metaDescription:"Free CTR calculator. Enter clicks and impressions to get your click-through rate, or fill any two of clicks, impressions and CTR to solve the third.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"CTR Calculator",heroLead:"Work out what share of your impressions actually clicked. 420 clicks from 30,000 impressions is a 1.4% click-through rate. Fill any two of clicks, impressions and CTR to get the third.",intro:["Click-through rate is clicks divided by impressions, expressed as a percent: 420 clicks from 30,000 impressions is 1.4%. It is the only number in an ad account that is purely about whether people wanted what they saw, because it has no money in it at all. Spend can be raised and audiences can be swapped, but the ratio only moves when the thing on screen becomes more worth tapping.","That makes CTR the cleanest read on creative you have. Two ads shown to the same audience at the same cost per thousand differ in exactly one respect, and the one with the higher click-through rate earned more attention from identical delivery. It is also why CTR is the first number to check after a creative refresh: it moves within days, long before conversions have accumulated enough volume to say anything reliable.","The calculator links the three figures both ways. Clicks and impressions give the rate of a campaign that already ran. A rate and an impression count project the clicks a plan produces. A rate and a click target tell you how many impressions that target needs, which is the version most people actually need when they are sizing a campaign. Fill any two boxes and the third fills itself in.","Read the number against the right comparison, though. All-industry blends put Meta near 1.4%, TikTok near 0.9%, and LinkedIn and Pinterest near 0.6%, while Google sits near 4.0% because that figure blends search intent with display. Comparing a feed ad to a search ad on click-through rate is comparing an interruption to an answer, and the interruption will lose every time without being worse."],tips:["Compare click-through rate within one placement and one format. Feed, Stories, search and display are different acts of attention, so a 1% feed rate and a 4% search rate are not evidence about your creative.","Use CTR as your fastest creative signal. It stabilises in days where cost per customer takes weeks, so it is the right number for deciding which of three new hooks survives the first week of testing.","Watch it fall as frequency climbs. A rate sliding while the same audience sees the same ad more often is fatigue, and no amount of bidding fixes an audience that has already decided.","Never optimise for click-through rate on its own. Clickbait raises it reliably and raises the bounce rate with it, and a campaign can post a record rate in the same month its cost per customer doubles.","Check that the promise in the ad matches the page it lands on. A mismatch shows up as a strong rate followed by a collapse in conversion rate, which is the most expensive way to be good at getting clicks.","Segment before you conclude anything. A blended account rate hides everything: one strong ad set and three weak ones can average out to a perfectly ordinary number that describes none of them."],faqs:[{question:"How do you calculate CTR?",answer:"CTR = clicks / impressions x 100. Divide clicks by impressions and multiply by a hundred to express it as a percent: 420 clicks from 30,000 impressions is a 1.4% click-through rate. Backwards it is a planning tool, since clicks = impressions x CTR / 100, so 200,000 impressions at 1.4% projects 2,800 clicks."},{question:"What is a good CTR?",answer:"It depends far more on where the ad runs than on how good it is. All-industry blended estimates put Meta near 1.4%, TikTok near 0.9%, and LinkedIn and Pinterest near 0.6%. Google sits near 4.0%, but that number blends search and display: search alone runs well above it because the person typed the query, and display alone runs under 0.5%. The useful comparison is your own rate last month on the same placement."},{question:"Why is my CTR high but my conversions low?",answer:"Because the ad is promising something the page does not deliver. A high rate with weak conversion is the signature of a mismatch: a curiosity hook, an offer that is only explained in the ad, or a landing page that answers a different question. It can also be simple friction, since a slow page loses a share of the people who clicked before they ever see it."},{question:"Does CTR affect what I pay?",answer:"Directly and twice. On a fixed cost per thousand impressions, every extra click is a discount on the cost of a visit, so doubling the rate halves the cost per click without touching the bid. Beyond that, the auction rewards ads people respond to, so a strong rate tends to buy cheaper delivery as well. That is why creative is the biggest cost lever in most accounts."},{question:"What is the difference between CTR and conversion rate?",answer:"Click-through rate measures the ad: how many of the people who saw it tapped. Conversion rate measures the page: how many of the people who arrived did the thing you wanted. They sit either side of the click and they fail for completely different reasons, which is why the pair together diagnoses an account that neither one does alone."},{question:"Is this calculator free, and is anything stored?",answer:"It is free, needs no account and runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you type is sent anywhere, there is no email step, and the benchmark table below is server rendered so the numbers are all readable before any JavaScript runs."}],relatedToolSlugs:["cpc-calculator","cpm-calculator","cpa-calculator","roas-calculator","ads-character-counter"]},"break-even-roas-calculator":{slug:"break-even-roas-calculator",family:"ad-metrics",platform:null,datePublished:m,lastUpdated:m,label:"Break-even ROAS calculator",metaTitle:"Break-Even ROAS Calculator (From Margin)",metaDescription:"Free break-even ROAS calculator. Enter your gross margin, or a price and unit cost, to see the return on ad spend where a campaign stops losing money.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"Break-Even ROAS Calculator",heroLead:"Your break-even ROAS is one divided by your gross margin. At a 40% margin that is 2.50x, at 20% it is 5.00x, at 80% it is 1.25x. Enter your margin, or a price and a unit cost, to find the line.",intro:["Break-even ROAS is the return on ad spend at which a campaign makes nothing and loses nothing, and it is decided by your margin rather than by your advertising. One divided by gross margin gives it: a 40% margin needs 2.50x, a 20% margin needs 5.00x, an 80% margin needs 1.25x. Every judgement about whether a campaign is working starts here, because without this line a return is just a number that went up or down.","The reason it is one over margin is easier to see in dollars. On a $60 order at a 40% margin, $24 of every sale is left after the cost of goods, so $60 of revenue can only carry $24 of ad spend before the sale is underwater. Sixty divided by twenty-four is 2.50, and that ratio holds at any order value because both sides scale together. Margin sets the line; the size of the basket does not.","This calculator works from either end. Enter a gross margin if you know it, or enter a price and a unit cost and it works the margin out, which is usually the faster route because most people can name what something sells for and what it costs to deliver long before they can name a percentage. Either way it prints the break-even multiple, and adds a target row for the profit you actually want on top of standing still.","That target row matters because break-even is not a goal, it is a floor. Wanting 20% profit on the spend at a 40% margin means (1 + 0.20) divided by 0.40, which is 3.00x rather than 2.50x. The gap between those two figures is the entire argument about whether an account is performing, and most accounts that feel like they are working while the bank balance disagrees are running somewhere between the two."],tips:["Use gross margin, not net. Cost of goods, payment fees, shipping and returns belong in the calculation because they scale with the order; rent and salaries do not and will make the line unreachable if you include them.","Recalculate after a discount. A 20% off code on a 40% margin product does not leave 20% margin, it leaves 25%, and the break-even multiple moves from 2.50x to 4.00x while every dashboard keeps reporting against the old target.","Set the working target above break-even on purpose. Wanting 20% profit on the spend at a 40% margin means aiming at 3.00x, and an account run at exactly break-even is one bad week away from having funded its own losses.","Convert the line into dollars for the people buying media. A ratio is hard to act on mid-campaign; average order value times margin, which is $24 on a $60 order at 40%, is a number a buyer can check a campaign against on a Tuesday.","Sanity check the attribution before trusting the comparison. Platforms report revenue generously to themselves, so a campaign clearing break-even on platform-reported revenue and missing it in your own analytics is the normal case rather than the exception.","Only pay above the first-order line if repeat purchases are measured. A subscription or a genuine repeat business can justify running under break-even on the first sale; a hope that customers will return cannot, and the difference is whether you can produce the number."],faqs:[{question:"What is break-even ROAS?",answer:"The return on ad spend where revenue exactly covers the cost of goods plus the ad spend, so the campaign neither makes nor loses money. It equals 1 divided by gross margin: 2.50x at a 40% margin, 5.00x at 20%, 1.25x at 80%. Anything above the line is profit on the spend, anything below it is a subsidy you are paying for the sale."},{question:"How do I work out my gross margin?",answer:"Subtract what one unit costs you to deliver from what it sells for, then divide by the price. A $60 product that costs $36 in goods, fees and shipping leaves $24, which is a 40% margin. Include everything that scales with the order, so payment processing, packaging, shipping and expected returns all count. Leave out fixed costs like rent, salaries and software, because they do not rise with the next sale."},{question:"What is a good target ROAS?",answer:"Break-even plus whatever profit you need the spend to produce. The formula is (1 + desired profit) / gross margin, so at a 40% margin with 20% profit on the spend the target is 3.00x against a 2.50x floor. This is why cross-industry target numbers are close to meaningless: a 3x target is ambitious on a 25% margin and unambitious on an 80% one."},{question:"Why is my 3x campaign still losing money?",answer:"Almost always one of three things. Your margin is thinner than the calculation assumed, often because discounts, returns or shipping were not counted. The reported revenue is not the revenue that landed, because the platform is attributing generously. Or the 3x is measuring ad spend alone while the business is also carrying agency fees, tooling and creative costs, which are real money the ratio never sees."},{question:"Should break-even ROAS or break-even CPA be my target?",answer:"They are the same line expressed in different units, so use whichever the people acting on it can check faster. Break-even ROAS is a ratio that works across products with different prices; break-even cost per acquisition, which is average order value times margin, is a dollar figure a media buyer can compare a campaign against directly. On a $60 order at a 40% margin they are 2.50x and $24, and they say exactly the same thing."},{question:"Is this calculator free, and is anything stored?",answer:"Free, ungated and entirely local. Your margin, price and cost never leave the browser, there is no email step, and the full margin to break-even table is printed on the page, so you can read the answer for any margin without typing anything."}],relatedToolSlugs:["cpa-calculator","roas-calculator","ads-budget-calculator","cpc-calculator","cpm-calculator"]},"qr-code-generator":{slug:"qr-code-generator",family:"qr-code",platform:null,datePublished:H,lastUpdated:H,label:"QR code generator",metaTitle:"Free QR Code Generator for Links, Profiles and Print",metaDescription:"Free QR code generator for links, profiles and packaging. Download a PNG at 512, 1024 or 2048 pixels or a vector SVG. Made in your browser, no tracking, no expiry.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"QR Code Generator",heroLead:"Paste a link and get a QR code you can print. PNG for slides, SVG for anything going to a printer, and the whole thing is encoded in your browser: nothing is uploaded and no scan is ever counted.",intro:["A QR code is the shortest route from something physical to something online. It turns a shop window, a coffee cup, a table tent, a booth banner or the last slide of a talk into a tap. For a social team it usually does one specific job: it gets someone from the thing in front of them to your profiles or your booking link without asking them to type a handle they will misspell.","This one encodes whatever text you paste, up to 213 bytes, which comfortably fits any URL you are likely to print. It uses error correction level M, the middle setting, which lets a scanner recover a symbol with roughly 15 percent of its area damaged: enough for a scuff on a sticker or a fold across a flyer. Download it as a PNG at 512, 1024 or 2048 pixels for slides and social posts, or as an SVG when it is going to a printer and needs to stay sharp at any size.","Point it at a page you control rather than at a single profile. Once a code is printed on a thousand coasters, the pattern is fixed forever, and the only thing you can still change is what sits at the far end of the link. A link-in-bio page is the usual answer: one URL on the print run, and behind it a page you can repoint at a new launch, a new booking form or a new profile whenever you want.","What this tool does not do is watch anybody. There is no redirect through our servers, no scan counter, no account step, and the link you type is never sent anywhere. The encoder is a plain function running in your tab, which also means the code keeps working if this page disappears tomorrow."],tips:["Keep the quiet zone. A QR code needs a clear margin of about four modules on every side so a scanner can tell where the symbol ends. Both downloads include it already, so place the file as it is rather than cropping tight to the pattern or letting a background photo run into the edge.","Dark on light, never the other way round. Most scanners expect dark modules on a light background, and a light code over a dark photo, a gradient or a busy image is the single most common reason a printed code fails. Solid white behind pure black is the safe choice, and it costs you nothing.","Print it at 2 cm across at the very least, which is about 0.8 inches. A rough rule for anything further away: allow about 1 cm of code for every 10 cm of scanning distance, so a poster read from two metres wants a code around 20 cm wide.","Test scan before the print run, not after. Open the plain camera app on two phones, one new and one old, and try it from the distance people will really stand at, in the light the code will really live in. A code that works on your desk at 10 cm can fail on a wall at 2 metres.","Short links make lighter codes. A 25 character URL fits a version 2 symbol with 25 modules per side, while a 120 character tracking URL needs version 7 at 45 modules per side, so every module is smaller and the code is harder to read at the same printed width. Strip the campaign parameters, or use a short link.",'Give the code something to say. A bare square gets ignored. One line under it, telling people what they get for scanning it, is worth more than any styling: "Scan for the menu" or "Scan to book" beats a code sitting on its own.'],faqs:[{question:"Does a QR code expire?",answer:"No. A QR code is not a record held on a server somewhere, it is your text encoded directly into the pattern of black and white squares. Nothing has to stay online for the code to keep working, no account has to stay active, and it cannot be switched off. Codes made here have no expiry because there is nothing to expire."},{question:"Can I change the link after the code is printed?",answer:"Not the code itself: the destination is baked into the pattern, so changing it means printing a new one. What you can change is where that URL leads, if the URL belongs to you. Point the code at your link-in-bio page and you can repoint that page at a new offer, a new form or a new profile as often as you like while the printed code stays valid. That is the reason to spend a moment on the destination before you print a thousand of anything."},{question:"Can I put my logo in the middle of the code?",answer:"Not in this version, and the reason is that it is a promise we cannot keep for every code. Level M error correction recovers about 15 percent of a damaged symbol, so a small logo often survives, but whether it does depends on the size of the logo, where it lands, and which modules it covers. We would rather not hand you a control that quietly produces codes failing on the one phone you did not test. Put the logo beside the code instead, in a caption or a frame, where it does the branding job without eating data."},{question:"Should I download the PNG or the SVG?",answer:"PNG if the code is going into a slide, a social post or an email, where a fixed pixel size is what everything else expects. SVG if it is going to a printer or into a design file: it is vector, so it stays perfectly sharp at any size, from a business card to a trade show banner, and it is a fraction of the file size. When in doubt for print, take the SVG."},{question:"Is my link sent to your servers?",answer:"No. The encoder runs entirely in your browser: your text becomes a grid of modules in the tab, the preview is drawn from that grid, and the PNG is rasterized on your own device. Nothing is uploaded, nothing is stored, and there is no redirect in the middle of the link, so we never see a single scan. That also means we cannot tell you how many people scanned it, which is the honest trade for not tracking anyone."},{question:"How long can the link be?",answer:"Up to 213 bytes of UTF-8, which is the capacity of the largest symbol offered here, version 10 at error correction level M. Practically every URL fits, including long ones with campaign parameters. Accented and non-Latin characters cost more than one byte each, so the counter on the page measures bytes rather than characters, and it shows which version your text selected as you type."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-image-resizer","social-media-post-preview","instagram-caption-spacer","social-media-fonts-generator"]},"hashtag-generator":{slug:"hashtag-generator",family:"hashtag-generator",platform:null,datePublished:S,lastUpdated:S,label:"Hashtag generator",metaTitle:"Hashtag Generator (Free Tags for 13 Industries)",metaDescription:"Free hashtag generator for 13 industries. Get 10 mixed-size tags on the page, plus the full 30 tag pack split into broad, medium and niche bands by email.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"Hashtag Generator",heroLead:"Pick your industry and get a hashtag set that is not 30 copies of the biggest tag in your niche. Ten tags free on the page, mixed across three size bands, and the full 30 tag pack sorted into broad, medium and niche by email.",intro:["Most hashtag generators hand you the largest tags they can find, which is the one thing you do not want. A tag carrying tens of millions of posts adds a few hundred impressions and then buries you: by the time you have refreshed the page, forty newer posts sit above yours, and nobody scrolls that far into a tag they were only browsing anyway.","This generator sorts every tag into three size bands instead, because the bands do different jobs. Broad tags are the reach ceiling, the ones that decide how big the room could theoretically get. Medium tags, roughly 100K to 1M posts, are where realistic discovery actually happens, because they are searched often enough to matter and small enough that a normal account can hold a top spot for more than a few seconds. Niche tags, under 100K posts, are where the community and the conversions live: fewer people, but the people who are there went looking for exactly this.","How many you use depends on where you are posting. Instagram allows 30 and rewards none of them for being there, so 5 to 15 tags that genuinely describe the post beat a 30 tag block every time. TikTok is closer to a search engine, so 3 to 6 tags in the caption, matched to the words someone would actually type, do more than a wall of trending tags. Both cases have the same rule underneath: every tag has to describe what is in the post, or it costs you rather than helping.","Every tag and every band here is a hand-curated estimate drawn from public hashtag research and platform browsing, last reviewed on the date at the top of this page. Volumes move constantly, so treat the tier as a guide to competition rather than a live count, and drop any tag that does not match what you actually made."],tips:["Delete any tag that does not describe the post in front of you. A set is not a costume: if the photo is a squat rack and the tag says nutrition, the people it reaches bounce, and a tag that reliably produces bounces stops sending you anyone.","Keep three or four sets and rotate them by post type, not one block you paste every time. Identical tag blocks on consecutive posts read as automation, and the rotation is free anyway once you have the pack.","Open a tag before you commit to it. Look at the top posts: if they are all from accounts fifty times your size, you will never hold a spot there, and if they are all spam or unrelated, the tag has been abandoned by the people you wanted.","Weight the set towards medium and niche. A workable split is two or three broad tags for the ceiling, four or five medium tags to do the work, and the rest niche, which is roughly how the free set on this page is mixed.","Avoid the tags that get posts quietly limited. Anything adjacent to weight loss, money-making claims, follow-for-follow or the generic engagement bait tags has been restricted at some point on some network, and a single restricted tag can hold back the whole post.","Check the reach figure in your insights a week later, not the likes on the day. Instagram and TikTok both report how much of a post was reached from hashtags or search, and that is the only number that tells you whether the set earned its place."],faqs:[{question:"How many hashtags should I actually use?",answer:"It depends on the network. On Instagram, 5 to 15 tags that genuinely describe the post outperform a 30 tag block: the limit is 30, but filling it is a signal of effort rather than relevance, and the extra 15 tags are almost always broad ones you cannot rank in. On TikTok, 3 to 6 is the working range, because the caption doubles as search text and every irrelevant tag dilutes what the post is about. On LinkedIn and X, three is plenty. The number matters far less than whether each tag matches the post."},{question:"What is the difference between broad, medium and niche tags?",answer:"Size, and therefore what each one is for. Broad tags carry roughly a million posts or more, so they set the reach ceiling but drop you out of the feed within minutes. Medium tags sit around 100K to 1M posts, big enough that people browse and search them and small enough that a normal account can rank in them, which makes them the tier that actually sends traffic. Niche tags are under 100K posts: the audience is small, but it is precisely the audience you wanted, which is why they convert well above their size. A 30 tag pack takes 10 from each band on purpose."},{question:"Do hashtags still work?",answer:"They work differently than they did. On Instagram they are now one relevance signal among several rather than the main distribution channel, and Instagram has said as much publicly, so a tag block will not rescue a post nobody watched. On TikTok they carry more weight because they feed search, and TikTok search is a genuine discovery surface. Everywhere, a small, accurate set helps the ranking system understand who a post is for, and a large, generic set mostly tells it nothing."},{question:"Where should I put my hashtags, in the caption or the first comment?",answer:"It makes no measurable difference to reach, so put them wherever the caption reads better. Tags in the first comment are indexed the same way as tags in the caption, which is why the first comment trick has survived: it keeps the caption clean rather than earning anything extra. On TikTok, put them in the caption, since the caption is also the text the search index reads."},{question:"Can a hashtag get my post hidden?",answer:"Yes, and it is the most common own goal in this whole area. Networks periodically restrict or unlist tags that attract policy-violating content, and the list is not published, so a tag that looked ordinary last year can be limited today. The pattern to watch is anything tied to weight loss claims, quick-money claims, follow-for-follow schemes or generic engagement bait. If reach on a post collapses for no visible reason, remove the tags one at a time before you rewrite anything else."},{question:"Do I get the full 30 tag pack?",answer:"Yes, by email. The 10 tag mixed set, the size band explanation and the worked example on this page are all free with no address at all. The full 30 tag pack, split into 10 broad, 10 medium and 10 niche for your industry, unlocks when you ask us to send it, and the emailed pack is built from exactly the same tag table you see here. There is no paid tier and no hidden data behind the form."}],relatedToolSlugs:["instagram-hashtag-generator","tiktok-hashtag-generator","social-media-fonts-generator","engagement-rate-calculator","content-calendar-generator","social-media-holidays"]},"instagram-hashtag-generator":{slug:"instagram-hashtag-generator",family:"hashtag-generator",platform:"instagram",datePublished:S,lastUpdated:S,label:"Instagram hashtag generator",metaTitle:"Instagram Hashtag Generator (Free Tags by Niche)",metaDescription:"Free Instagram hashtag generator. Get 10 mixed-size tags for your niche instantly, plus the full 30 tag pack split into broad, medium and niche bands by email.",heroEyebrow:"Free Instagram tool",heroTitle:"Instagram Hashtag Generator",heroLead:"Build an Instagram hashtag set worth pasting. Ten tags free on the page, mixed across three size bands, and the full 30 tag pack by email. Use 5 to 15 of them, not all 30.",intro:["Instagram lets you attach 30 hashtags and gives you nothing for using all of them. The 30 tag block is a habit left over from 2016, when tags were the feed, and it survives because it feels like effort. What it usually produces now is a set where the first four tags are so large that your post is gone before anyone loads the page, and the remaining twenty-six describe a post you did not make.","What still works on Instagram is a short, accurate set that helps the ranking system place your post with the right people. Reels and the Explore grid are where a stranger finds you, and both of those surfaces are driven by whether people watch, save and send the post once it arrives. Hashtags do not override that, but they do decide which audience gets the first chance to save it, and putting your post in front of a room that cares is how a save rate stays high enough to keep the distribution going.","That is why the set this generator builds is weighted rather than sorted by size. Broad tags carry roughly a million posts or more and give you the ceiling. Medium tags, around 100K to 1M, are the ones a normal account can actually rank in and the ones people genuinely browse. Niche tags, under 100K, are small rooms full of exactly the right people, and on Instagram they are where saves and follows come from rather than passing likes.","Every tag here is a hand-curated estimate from public hashtag research and browsing, not measured Instagram data and never anything about an individual account. Post volumes move every week, so use the band as a guide to how much competition you are walking into, and cut any tag that does not honestly describe the photo, carousel or Reel it is attached to."],tips:["Use 5 to 15 tags, not 30. The extra tags are almost always broad ones where you cannot hold a place for more than a minute, and a tighter set makes the relevance signal cleaner rather than louder.","Pick the set for the individual post, not for the account. A gym account posting a recipe should tag the recipe. The people who arrive from a food tag and save it are worth more than the people who arrive from a training tag and scroll past.","Optimise for saves and sends rather than likes. Those two actions are what push a Reel or a carousel into Explore, so choose the tags where a post like yours gets saved, which is usually the niche band rather than the broad one.","Put the tags at the end of the caption or in the first comment, whichever reads better. Both are indexed the same way, so this is a formatting choice: if you want the caption to end on a sentence rather than a wall of blue, use the first comment or push them below the line break.","Add one tag you own and use it on every post. A branded tag collects your own archive, gives customers somewhere to post their photos, and is the only tag in the set where you are guaranteed the top spot.","Drop anything near the restricted list. Weight loss claims, follow-for-follow, like-for-like and the generic engagement bait tags have all been limited at some point, they are not published anywhere, and one of them is enough to hold back a post that was otherwise fine."],faqs:[{question:"How many hashtags should I use on Instagram?",answer:"Between 5 and 15, chosen for the specific post. Instagram allows 30 and there is no penalty for using them, but there is also no reward: the last fifteen tags in a maxed-out block are nearly always broad ones you cannot rank in, and they make it harder rather than easier for the system to work out who the post is for. A short set weighted towards medium and niche tags consistently beats a long generic one."},{question:"Do hashtags still work on Instagram in 2026?",answer:"They work as a relevance signal, not as a distribution channel. Instagram has been explicit that hashtags do not meaningfully expand reach on their own, and the surfaces that actually grow an account are Reels and Explore, which rank on watch time, saves and sends. What hashtags still do is help Instagram place your post with an audience likely to take those actions, which is worth real reach indirectly. Treat them as targeting rather than as a growth lever."},{question:"Should hashtags go in the caption or the first comment?",answer:"Either. Tags in the first comment are indexed exactly like tags in the caption, so the choice is purely about how the caption reads. The first comment keeps the post looking clean in the feed, which is why the practice stuck around, but nobody has shown it earns extra reach. If you keep them in the caption, push them below a few line breaks so the opening line still does its job."},{question:"Can hashtags get my Instagram post shadowbanned?",answer:"A restricted tag can limit a post, yes. Instagram periodically unlists or restricts tags that attract policy-violating content, does not publish the list, and a tag that was ordinary last year can be limited now. The recurring offenders cluster around weight loss claims, get-rich claims, follow-for-follow, like-for-like and generic engagement bait. If a post loses reach for no visible reason, remove the hashtags first and check whether the next post recovers."},{question:"Are the same hashtags good for Reels and feed posts?",answer:"Mostly, with one adjustment. Reels reach people who do not follow you far more often than a feed photo does, so a Reel can afford one or two broader tags to widen the top of the funnel. Feed posts and carousels do better when the whole set leans niche, because their value on Instagram is saves from people who already care rather than raw impressions from people passing through."},{question:"Do I get the full 30 tag pack?",answer:"Yes, by email. The 10 tag mixed set, the size band explanation and the worked example on this page are free with no address. The full pack, 10 broad, 10 medium and 10 niche tags for your industry, unlocks when you ask us to send it, and the emailed version is built from exactly the same tag table shown here."}],relatedToolSlugs:["hashtag-generator","tiktok-hashtag-generator","instagram-caption-spacer","best-time-to-post-on-instagram","instagram-engagement-rate-calculator","social-media-fonts-generator"]},"tiktok-hashtag-generator":{slug:"tiktok-hashtag-generator",family:"hashtag-generator",platform:"tiktok",datePublished:S,lastUpdated:S,label:"TikTok hashtag generator",metaTitle:"TikTok Hashtag Generator (Free Tags for Search)",metaDescription:"Free TikTok hashtag generator. Get 10 mixed-size tags for your niche instantly, plus the full 30 tag pack split into broad, medium and niche bands by email.",heroEyebrow:"Free TikTok tool",heroTitle:"TikTok Hashtag Generator",heroLead:"Build a TikTok hashtag set around what people search, not what is trending. Ten tags free on the page, mixed across three size bands, and the full 30 tag pack by email. Use 3 to 6 in the caption.",intro:["TikTok hashtags do a different job from Instagram hashtags, and copying the Instagram habit across is why so many captions look like a tag dump. On TikTok the caption is search text. It is read by the search index, it decides which queries your video can answer, and a huge share of TikTok usage is now people typing a question into the search bar rather than waiting for the For You feed to serve them something.","So the useful question is not which tags are trending, it is which words someone would type when they want the thing in your video. A tag that matches a real query puts you in a result set that keeps returning people for months, long after the For You push has finished. A tag that only rides a trend gives you one afternoon in a room that has already moved on.","The For You feed is the second half of the picture, and it is mostly driven by watch time, rewatches and shares rather than by tags. Hashtags feed it the topic, not the verdict: they tell TikTok what the video is about so it can find the first hundred people likely to finish it, and everything after that is decided by whether those people did. A short, accurate set of 3 to 6 tags is enough for that, and it leaves the caption readable enough to carry a hook.","Every tag and every size band here is a hand-curated estimate from public hashtag research and platform browsing, not measured TikTok data and never anything about an individual account. Volumes move constantly, so read the band as a competition guide and cut any tag that does not describe what is actually on screen."],tips:["Use 3 to 6 tags and spend the rest of the caption on words people search. The caption is indexed, so a sentence naming the thing your video shows is worth more than four extra tags naming the category it belongs to.","Say the keyword out loud and put it on screen as text. TikTok reads spoken words and on-screen text as well as the caption, so a video that says what it is beats one that only tags what it is, and the three signals reinforce each other.","Skip the reflex tags. Adding the biggest generic tags in the app to every video tells TikTok nothing about the video, and it competes you against everything posted in the last ten seconds instead of the handful of videos that answer the same question.","Only ride a trending tag when the video genuinely fits it. Trend tags bring a burst of the wrong audience if the video does not deliver, and TikTok reads a fast scroll away as the strongest negative signal it has.","Lean into the niche band harder than you would on Instagram. Under 100K posts on TikTok usually means a specific search query, and a video that stays the best answer to a specific query keeps collecting views for months.","Avoid tags tied to restricted claims. Weight loss promises, money-making promises and engagement bait tags are the ones most likely to get a video quietly limited or held out of search, and there is no notification when it happens."],faqs:[{question:"How many hashtags should I use on TikTok?",answer:"Three to six. TikTok captions are limited and doubly valuable, since the same text is read by search, so every tag you add costs you room for words a person might actually type. A tight set of three specific tags plus a descriptive sentence outperforms ten tags almost every time, because it gives the search index something precise to match against."},{question:"Do hashtags matter for the For You page?",answer:"Indirectly. The For You ranking is driven by watch time, rewatches, shares and comments, so tags do not decide whether a video takes off. What they do is tell TikTok what the video is about, which shapes who gets shown it first. Getting that initial audience right matters a lot, because a well-matched first hundred viewers finish the video and an unmatched hundred scroll away, and the system reads that difference immediately."},{question:"Should I use trending hashtags on TikTok?",answer:"Only when the video genuinely belongs to the trend. A trending tag on an unrelated video buys you a wave of people who swipe past in a second, and fast swipes are the clearest negative signal TikTok collects. Riding a sound or a format you actually participated in is a different matter, and there the tag is simply accurate."},{question:"How does TikTok search change how I pick tags?",answer:"It makes the phrasing matter more than the size. TikTok has become a place people search rather than only browse, and search results keep sending views for months after a video posts, so the highest value tag is often a small one that exactly matches a question. Write down the sentence someone would type to find your video, then choose the tags and the caption that contain those words."},{question:"Can hashtags get a TikTok video limited?",answer:"Yes. TikTok restricts tags associated with policy-violating content, does not publish the list, and applies limits without notifying you. The usual suspects are weight loss and body claims, quick-money claims and follow-for-follow style engagement bait. If a video underperforms your baseline badly and nothing else changed, strip the tags and watch whether the next upload recovers."},{question:"Do I get the full 30 tag pack?",answer:"Yes, by email. The 10 tag mixed set, the size band explanation and the worked example on this page are free with no address. The full pack, 10 broad, 10 medium and 10 niche tags for your industry, unlocks when you ask us to send it, and the emailed version comes from exactly the same tag table shown here."}],relatedToolSlugs:["hashtag-generator","instagram-hashtag-generator","best-time-to-post-on-tiktok","tiktok-engagement-rate-calculator","tiktok-character-counter","social-media-fonts-generator"]},"instagram-caption-generator":{slug:"instagram-caption-generator",family:"caption-generator",platform:"instagram",datePublished:b,lastUpdated:b,label:"Instagram caption generator",metaTitle:"Instagram Caption Generator (Free Captions by Goal)",metaDescription:"Free Instagram caption generator for 13 industries. Three ready captions on the page, built from a hook, a body and one call to action, plus the full 30 caption pack by email.",heroEyebrow:"Free Instagram tool",heroTitle:"Instagram Caption Generator",heroLead:"Pick your industry and what the post is meant to do, and get captions that are already built: a first line that stops the scroll, a middle that earns the save, and one clear ask at the end. Three free on the page, the full 30 caption pack by email.",intro:["A blank caption box is a worse problem than a bad caption. Most people who sit in front of one do not run out of ideas, they run out of a starting shape: they know roughly what the post is about, they have no idea what the first line should be, and twenty minutes later the caption reads like a description of the photo. The photo already did that job. The caption has a different one.","Every caption this generator writes has the same three parts, because those three parts are the whole job. The hook is the first line, and it is the only part most people will ever read, because Instagram folds the caption after roughly two lines and shows the rest behind a more link. The body is what a person finds when they tap that link, and it has to be worth the tap. The call to action is the last line, and it names the single thing you want the reader to do next, which is the part that gets cut most often and matters most.","The three goals change all three parts, not just the ending. An engagement caption opens with something arguable and closes with a question you would actually answer in the replies. A sales caption opens by naming a problem the reader recognises and closes with the specific next step, a link, a word to comment, a DM. A growth caption opens by telling a first time visitor what this account is, and closes by asking them to follow. Picking the wrong goal is how you end up with a beautifully written caption that asks a stranger to book a call.","These are templates, and templates are a starting structure rather than a finished caption. The wording here is editorial, written for Instagram and distilled from public copywriting patterns, not measured Postify data and never anything about an individual account. Paste one, then change the words that do not sound like you, add the detail only you have, and cut whatever is left over. The generator saves you the blank page, not the thinking."],tips:["Spend most of your editing time on the first line. Instagram folds the caption after about two lines, so the hook is the entire caption for most of the people who scroll past it. If the first line could sit under any post in your niche, it is not a hook yet.","Ask for one thing at the end, not three. A caption that asks for a comment, a save and a follow gets none of them. Pick the single action that matches the goal you generated for, and let the other two go.","Put the detail only you have into the body. The template gives you the shape of a sentence, and the number, the name, the week it went wrong or the thing a customer said out loud is what turns it into your post. That detail is also the part nobody else can generate.","Read the caption out loud before you publish it. Anything you would not say in a voice note comes out in that read, and it is usually the sentence that sounds most like marketing. Cut it and the caption gets shorter and better at the same time.","Match the caption goal to the post, not to the month. A promotional caption under a behind the scenes clip reads as a bait and switch, and the engagement version of the same caption under a launch post wastes the launch. Generate the goal that the picture is already making a case for.","Keep a running note of the hooks that worked. Two or three first lines will outperform everything else you post, and once you know which ones they are, the generator is mostly a way of producing more captions in that shape rather than a way of finding new ones."],faqs:[{question:"How long should an Instagram caption be?",answer:"As long as the idea needs and no longer, which in practice means one of two shapes. A short caption of one or two lines works when the image carries the meaning and the caption only has to add the ask. A long caption works when there is a story or a genuine teaching point in it, and there is no penalty for length, only for padding. What matters far more than the total is the first line, because Instagram folds the caption after roughly two lines. If you want to see exactly where your caption gets cut on each network, that is what our character counter is for."},{question:"Are generated captions bad for reach?",answer:"Nothing about a template is visible to Instagram. What hurts reach is a caption that says nothing, and that happens just as often when a person writes it from scratch at eleven at night. The real risk with any generator is sameness: if you paste the output unedited, your account starts to sound like every other account using the same tool. The fix is the last ten percent, which is why every caption here is built to be edited rather than published as it stands."},{question:"What is the hook, body and CTA structure?",answer:"It is the three jobs a caption has to do, separated so you can do them one at a time. The hook is the opening line, and it earns the tap on more. The body is the middle, and it pays that tap back with something useful, honest or entertaining. The call to action is the closing line, and it names one next step. Every caption this tool produces is a hook plus a body plus a CTA joined by a blank line, which is also why they are easy to edit: you can swap one part without rewriting the other two."},{question:"How do I make a template sound like me?",answer:"Change the nouns and cut the qualifiers. The template gives you a sentence that works structurally, and it stays generic until you put your own specifics into it: the number, the client, the mistake, the thing that happened on Tuesday. After that, delete the words that soften the claim, since almost every generic caption is a good sentence with three hedges bolted onto it. Two passes and it is yours."},{question:"Should I use emoji and line breaks in my captions?",answer:"Emoji, yes, sparingly and where they replace a word rather than decorate one. Line breaks are worth more: a caption written as one block gets skimmed, and the same caption split into short paragraphs gets read. Instagram is awkward about keeping those breaks when you paste from another app, which is a formatting problem rather than a writing one, and our caption spacer exists purely to solve it."},{question:"Do I get the full 30 caption pack?",answer:"Yes, by email. The three captions on the page and the full anatomy breakdown below are free with no address at all. The complete 30 caption pack for your industry and goal unlocks when you ask us to send it, and the emailed pack is generated from exactly the same template table you just used, so the version in your inbox is the one on your screen. There is no paid tier and nothing extra hidden behind the form."}],relatedToolSlugs:["instagram-caption-spacer","instagram-character-counter","instagram-hashtag-generator","content-calendar-generator","instagram-post-preview","social-media-fonts-generator"]},"instagram-bio-generator":{slug:"instagram-bio-generator",family:"bio-generator",platform:"instagram",datePublished:f,lastUpdated:f,label:"Instagram bio generator",metaTitle:"Instagram Bio Generator (Free Bio Ideas in 150 Characters)",metaDescription:"Free Instagram bio generator for 13 industries. Three bios on the page in three tones, every one inside the 150 character limit, plus the full 25 bio pack by email.",heroEyebrow:"Free Instagram tool",heroTitle:"Instagram Bio Generator",heroLead:"Instagram gives you 150 characters to explain who you are and why a stranger should stay. Pick your industry and a tone, get three bios that already fit, and the full 25 bio pack by email.",intro:["The Instagram bio is the only part of your profile that a visitor reads on purpose. Everything else they scan: the grid, the numbers, the profile picture. The bio is where they decide whether following you is worth the room in their feed, and that decision usually takes about three seconds, which is roughly one line of text.","You have 150 characters to spend, and line breaks count as characters, so a bio laid out over three lines has already spent some of the budget on white space. That constraint is the whole design problem. A 150 character bio cannot list five services, three qualifications and a location. It can name what you do, say who it is for, and point at one next step, and a bio that does those three things beats a longer one that does none of them clearly.","The field itself is not the only text on your profile, and this is the part most people leave on the table. The name field, the one directly above the bio, is separate, it holds a different set of characters, and it is the part Instagram search actually reads alongside your username. A name field that says a real name only is a name field doing half its job: putting the thing you do in it, next to your name, is the single cheapest discovery change available on the profile. The category label under it, the one you set from a list rather than typing, does similar work for free.","Every bio this generator produces fits inside 150 characters after your industry and topic have been substituted in, so nothing gets cut when you paste it. The wording is editorial, written from public bio conventions on Instagram rather than measured Postify data, and the three tones exist because the same information sounds completely different depending on whether you are a consultancy, a person posting from a phone, or an account that would rather repel the wrong follower than keep everyone."],tips:["Write the name field before you write the bio. It is a separate field from the bio, Instagram search reads it, and most accounts waste it on a name that is already in the username. Your name plus the thing you do fits comfortably and is worth more than any sentence in the bio itself.","Say who it is for, not only what you do. A bio that says photographer competes with every photographer. A bio that says photographer for restaurants that hate their menu photos gets skipped by everybody except the person who was looking for exactly that, which is the entire point.","Spend the last line on one next step. One link, one action, one instruction. Instagram gives you a single clickable link, so if you need several destinations put a link in bio page behind it rather than listing three URLs nobody can tap.","Set the category label. It sits under the name, it costs nothing, it does not eat any of your 150 characters, and it tells both Instagram and a first time visitor what kind of account this is before they have read a word.","Treat highlights as the paragraph the bio does not have room for. Pricing, FAQs, results, start here: the covers are what a visitor taps when the bio has interested them but not convinced them, and a bio written to be short works far better when there is somewhere for the detail to live.","Keep the bio boring in the useful places. Clever wording that leaves a stranger unsure what you actually do is the most common bio failure, and the second most common is filling the field with emoji at the expense of the sentence that would have explained it."],faqs:[{question:"How many characters can an Instagram bio be?",answer:"One hundred and fifty, and line breaks count towards that total. It is a hard ceiling rather than a fold: the field simply stops accepting input, so nothing gets truncated after the fact. Every bio this generator produces is already inside the limit for the industry you picked, which is why the tool shows a live character count next to each one rather than making you paste it in to find out."},{question:"What should I put in the Instagram name field?",answer:"Your name and the thing you do. The name field is separate from both the username and the bio, it has its own allowance, and it is one of the few pieces of profile text that Instagram search matches against, so a bakery whose name field reads the bakery name plus the word bakery and the city is findable in a way that a bare name is not. This is not a trick and it is not keyword stuffing: it is filling in a field that most accounts leave half empty."},{question:"How many links can I put in an Instagram bio?",answer:"Instagram allows more than one link on a profile now, but the practical answer has not changed much: visitors tap the first thing they see, and a profile offering five destinations converts worse than one offering the single destination that matters this month. If you genuinely need several, point the bio at one landing page and let that page do the branching, which is what a link in bio page is for."},{question:"Should my Instagram bio use emoji and line breaks?",answer:"Sparingly, and only where they replace a word. Every emoji costs at least one character out of 150 and some cost two, and a bio built out of symbols is harder to read than the sentence it replaced, not easier. Line breaks are useful for separating what you do from what to do next, but each one is also a character, so a three line bio is spending part of its budget on layout. Start from the words and add the decoration afterwards if there is room."},{question:"How often should I change my bio?",answer:"The core sentence should hold for months, because it is the answer to what this account is, and that should not change every week. The last line is the part worth rotating: the offer, the launch, the current link. A practical rhythm is to leave the first line alone and rewrite the call to action whenever what you are pointing people at changes."},{question:"Do I get the full 25 bio pack?",answer:"Yes, by email. The three bios on the page, the character counts and the full comparison below are free with no address. The complete 25 bio pack for your industry and tone unlocks when you ask us to send it, and the emailed pack is built from exactly the same template table you just used, so every bio in it fits the same 150 characters."}],relatedToolSlugs:["tiktok-bio-generator","instagram-character-counter","instagram-caption-generator","instagram-hashtag-generator","social-media-fonts-generator","instagram-post-preview"]},"tiktok-bio-generator":{slug:"tiktok-bio-generator",family:"bio-generator",platform:"tiktok",datePublished:f,lastUpdated:f,label:"TikTok bio generator",metaTitle:"TikTok Bio Generator (Free Bio Ideas in 80 Characters)",metaDescription:"Free TikTok bio generator for 13 industries. Three bios on the page in three tones, every one inside the brutal 80 character limit, plus the full 25 bio pack by email.",heroEyebrow:"Free TikTok tool",heroTitle:"TikTok Bio Generator",heroLead:"TikTok gives you 80 characters. That is one sentence, so it has to be the right one. Pick your industry and a tone, get three bios that already fit, and the full 25 bio pack by email.",intro:["Eighty characters is not a short bio, it is a headline. Instagram allows 150 and TikTok allows 80, and the gap between those two numbers is the difference between a bio that can name what you do and who it is for, and a bio that gets to make exactly one claim. Almost every bad TikTok bio is an Instagram bio that was pasted in and then hacked at until it fit, which is why so many of them end mid thought.","The right approach is to start from the constraint rather than fight it. Pick the single thing you want to be known for, write it as one sentence a stranger could repeat back to you, and stop. Every bio this generator produces was written to that rule, not trimmed down to it: the TikTok template set is a completely separate set of strings from the Instagram one, because an 80 character bio has room for one claim and the sentence has to be built that way from the start.","The bio is also search text. TikTok search is a genuine discovery surface rather than a convenience feature, and profiles surface in it, so the words in your bio and your name are part of how somebody looking for what you do actually finds you. That does not mean stuffing keywords into 80 characters. It means that when you have a choice between a clever phrase and the plain word a person would type, the plain word usually wins, and the clever phrase belongs in a video instead.","One more thing shapes what a TikTok bio can do: the clickable website field is not available to every account. Plenty of profiles have no link at all, which is why so many TikTok bios spell out a handle, an email or a plain domain as text instead. Write the bio so it still works without a tappable link, and treat the link as an upgrade rather than the plan. Everything here is editorial, written from public bio conventions on TikTok, and every generated bio fits the 80 characters without truncation."],tips:["Make one claim, not three. Eighty characters is roughly one sentence, and the bios that read well spend all of it saying a single thing clearly rather than fitting three fragments in with commas between them.","Use the words a person would search. TikTok profiles surface in search, so a bio containing the plain description of what you do is doing quiet work every day, while a bio built entirely out of an inside joke is doing none.","Count the emoji. At 80 characters an emoji is an expensive decoration, and two of them can be the difference between a sentence that fits and one that gets cut. If a symbol replaces a word, keep it. If it decorates one, drop it.","Do not write the bio around a link you may not have. The website field is not on every account, so a bio whose whole call to action is the word below fails silently for anyone who cannot see a link. Put the destination in words if it matters.","Let the videos carry the personality. On Instagram the bio does more of the work because the grid is quiet. On TikTok a visitor arrives having already watched something of yours, so the bio only has to confirm what they just worked out.","Check the bio on a phone, not on the desktop site. The profile layout is narrower than you think and a bio that fits in the field can still wrap awkwardly, which is worth the ten seconds it takes to look."],faqs:[{question:"How many characters can a TikTok bio be?",answer:"Eighty, which is roughly one sentence. It is a hard ceiling: the field stops accepting input rather than truncating what you wrote, so a bio pasted in from somewhere longer simply arrives cut off at the wrong place. Every bio this generator produces is inside 80 characters for the industry you picked, and the tool shows the count next to each one."},{question:"Why is the TikTok bio limit so much shorter than Instagram?",answer:"Because the two profiles do different jobs. An Instagram profile is often the first thing a stranger sees, so the bio has to introduce the account from scratch inside 150 characters. A TikTok visitor almost always arrives from a video they already watched, so the bio is a confirmation rather than an introduction, and 80 characters is enough to confirm something. It does mean an Instagram bio cannot be reused here, which is why the template sets are separate rather than shared."},{question:"Can I put a link in my TikTok bio?",answer:"Only if your account has the website field, and not every account does. Where it exists it is a single clickable link and it behaves like the Instagram one, so point it at one destination rather than a list. Where it does not exist yet, write the destination out as plain text, and remember that a written address cannot be tapped, so it has to be short enough to remember or type."},{question:"Do keywords in a TikTok bio help me get found?",answer:"Plain words help, keyword stuffing does not. TikTok search returns profiles as well as videos, and the text on your profile is part of what it matches against, so using the ordinary phrase somebody would type is worth more than a stylised version of it. Cramming three keywords into 80 characters produces a bio that reads like a search query and convinces nobody who lands on it, which costs you the follow you were trying to earn."},{question:"What should a TikTok bio actually say?",answer:"What you post about, and who it is for, in one sentence. That is the whole brief at this length. The tone is where the room is: the same fact can be said as a credential, as a friendly aside, or as a challenge, and the three tones in this generator exist to show you the same claim in all three voices before you pick one."},{question:"Do I get the full 25 bio pack?",answer:"Yes, by email. The three bios on the page, the character counts and the comparison below are free with no address. The complete 25 bio pack for your industry and tone unlocks when you ask us to send it, and every bio in the emailed pack is built from the same template table and fits the same 80 characters."}],relatedToolSlugs:["instagram-bio-generator","tiktok-character-counter","tiktok-hashtag-generator","video-hook-generator","best-time-to-post-on-tiktok","social-media-fonts-generator"]},"video-hook-generator":{slug:"video-hook-generator",family:"hook-generator",platform:null,datePublished:_,lastUpdated:_,label:"Video hook generator",metaTitle:"Video Hook Generator (100 Free Opening Lines)",metaDescription:"Free video hook generator for Reels, Shorts and TikTok. Five opening lines on the page across five hook patterns, plus the full 100 hook pack for your industry by email.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"Video Hook Generator",heroLead:"The first three seconds decide whether the rest of the video happens. Pick your industry and get opening lines built from ten hook patterns that work on Reels, Shorts and TikTok. Five free here, all 100 by email.",intro:["Short video is decided at the top. A viewer scrolling a vertical feed makes a stay or go decision before your intro music finishes, and every ranking system in the category reads that decision immediately: how many people were still there at three seconds, how many watched to the end, how many watched twice. Nothing you do later in the video can rescue a first line that gave people permission to swipe.","That is why the opening line is a separate craft from the rest of the script. Most videos that underperform do not have a content problem, they have a first three seconds problem: they start with a greeting, a logo, a slow pan, or a sentence explaining what the video is going to be about instead of being about it. Every one of those is a retention cliff, and the drop happens in the exact window that decides how far the video travels.","The ten patterns in this generator are the shapes that survive that window. An open loop promises something and withholds it. A pattern interrupt breaks the scroll rhythm with a hard stop. A contrarian take disagrees with the thing the viewer has heard a hundred times. Stakes name what they lose by getting it wrong. Each one is a different reason to stay for another second, and having ten of them matters more than having one good one, because the same shape repeated on every video stops working on the people who watch all of them.","These lines are written to be said out loud in the first second, before any greeting and before any logo, and the strongest videos usually put the same words on screen at the same time so the line works with the sound off. Everything here is editorial, distilled from public short form video patterns and platform best practice rather than measured Postify data. They are openings, not scripts: whatever the hook promises, the rest of the video has to pay it back, and a hook that oversells is a faster way to lose an audience than a boring one."],tips:["Say the hook in the first second, before the greeting. Hey guys, welcome back is the most expensive two seconds in short video, because it is spent in the exact window that decides whether the rest gets watched.","Put the hook on screen as text as well as saying it. A large share of the feed watches muted at first, so a spoken hook with no on-screen text simply does not exist for those viewers, and the two together are stronger than either alone.","Cut the first three seconds of your last video and watch what happens. Most videos are better with the opening removed, because the real hook was the sentence that came after the throat clearing.","Pay the hook back. Every one of these lines makes a promise, and a video that opens on a promise it never keeps trains people to swipe on your next one. Overselling costs more than a flat opening does.","Rotate the patterns rather than finding one that works and repeating it. Somebody who watches five of your videos in a week notices when all five open the same way, and the shape stops surprising them, which is the only thing it was doing.","Match the hook to the payoff you actually have. A stakes hook on a light video reads as manipulation, and a listicle promise you do not deliver in the video is the fastest way to lose a rewatch, so pick the pattern that fits the footage you already shot."],faqs:[{question:"What makes a good video hook?",answer:"It gives the viewer a reason to stay that lands before they have decided to leave, and it does that in one sentence. In practice, three things separate a hook that works from one that does not: it starts immediately rather than after an introduction, it is specific enough that the viewer knows what they are staying for, and the video actually delivers on it. Every pattern in this generator is a different way of doing the first two, and only you can do the third."},{question:"How long do I have before someone scrolls?",answer:"Less time than feels reasonable. The stay or go decision on a vertical feed happens in the first seconds, well before most creators have finished their opening sentence, and everything that decides how far a video travels is measured from there: the drop at three seconds, the share of the video watched, the rewatch. Treat the opening line as its own deliverable and write it before the rest of the script rather than after."},{question:"Do the same hooks work on Reels, Shorts and TikTok?",answer:"Yes, which is why this tool has one page rather than three. All three surfaces are vertical, sound on by default for some viewers and muted for others, and ranked mostly on watch through and repeat views, so the mechanics of the first three seconds are the same everywhere. What differs is style and length rather than structure: TikTok tolerates a rougher, faster opening, and a Reel or a Short with the same line simply looks a little more produced."},{question:"Should the hook be spoken or on screen?",answer:"Both, saying the same thing. Feeds autoplay muted for many viewers, so an on-screen text hook reaches people the spoken one never does, and a spoken hook holds the people who already have sound on. Where creators go wrong is writing two different hooks, one for the caption text and one for the voice, which splits the viewer attention instead of doubling it. Same words, same second."},{question:"Can I reuse the same hook on different videos?",answer:"You can reuse a pattern, and you should. Reusing the exact line is what makes a feed of your videos feel like a template, and the people most likely to notice are the ones who watch you most. The useful habit is to keep the ten shapes in rotation and write a fresh line inside whichever shape suits the video, which is exactly what having 100 lines across ten patterns is for."},{question:"Do I get the full 100 hook pack?",answer:"Yes, by email. The five hooks on the page, one from each of the first five patterns, and the full explanation of all ten patterns below, are free with no address. The complete 100 hook pack for your industry, ten lines in each of the ten patterns, unlocks when you ask us to send it, and it is built from exactly the same pattern table you can read on this page."}],relatedToolSlugs:["content-calendar-generator","instagram-caption-generator","tiktok-hashtag-generator","thread-splitter","social-media-video-size-checker","best-time-to-post-on-tiktok"]},"social-media-roi-calculator":{slug:"social-media-roi-calculator",family:"roi-calculator",platform:null,datePublished:$,lastUpdated:$,label:"Social media ROI calculator",metaTitle:"Social Media ROI Calculator (Free, Time and Tools)",metaDescription:"Free social media ROI calculator. Price the hours your team spends, add tool costs, set both against attributed revenue, get the breakdown by email.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"Social Media ROI Calculator",heroLead:"Work out what social media actually costs you every month, hours included, and what it returns against the revenue you can credibly attribute to it. The return on your total cost is free on this page. The full breakdown, including the hours a scheduling workflow gives back, comes by email.",intro:["Ask most teams what social media costs and the answer is a subscription line: a scheduler, maybe a design tool, forty or eighty dollars a month. That figure is almost never the cost. The cost is the hours, and the hours stay invisible because nobody invoices for them. The afternoon spent writing five captions, the twenty minutes lost re-uploading the same video to a third network, the Sunday evening spent catching up because Thursday got away from someone. Price those hours properly and the monthly total usually moves by an order of magnitude, which is the entire reason this calculator exists.","The arithmetic is the one a finance team would use. Weekly hours become monthly hours by multiplying by 4.345, the average number of weeks in a month rather than the four everyone reaches for, and those monthly hours are valued at whatever an hour of that person is worth: a loaded salary divided by 2,080, a contractor rate, or the rate you would bill a client for the same work. Tools go on top. What comes out is the fully loaded monthly cost of the channel, and every other number on this page is measured against it.","The revenue side is where a return calculation usually goes wrong, so treat it strictly. Attributed revenue is what you can credibly credit to social: orders that arrived on a tracked link, sign ups that came through the profile link, deals where the buyer told you where they found you. It is not the revenue of the whole business in a month during which social media happened to exist. Anything you cannot trace counts as zero here, which makes the first result feel harsh and makes it honest. When that number comes out uncomfortably low, the fix is almost always tracking rather than effort.","Notice what is deliberately absent: impressions, followers and reach appear nowhere in the calculation. A month of excellent reach that produced no traceable revenue is a cost with no return, and treating reach as the outcome is the most common way a social budget survives three years without anyone asking what it earned. The last two lines of the result take the other route out of a bad number. Roughly 30 percent of hands-on posting time is repetition rather than creative work, so the calculator prices what batching and scheduling would hand back. That share is an editorial estimate covering repetition only: filming, writing and editing take exactly as long either way."],tips:["Count the hours before you try to cut them. An hour nobody has written down is an hour nobody can save, and in most teams the honest weekly figure is two to three times the one people quote from memory.","Use a loaded hourly rate rather than a bare salary divide. Salary divided by 2,080 working hours is the floor; adding 25 to 30 percent for payroll taxes, benefits and overhead gets you to what the hour genuinely costs the business.","Count everyone who touches the channel, not only the person who publishes. The designer who cuts the vertical version, the founder who approves captions on a Friday and the intern who answers comments are all real cost sitting outside the marketing headcount.","Put tracked links on every profile, bio and campaign destination before you trust the revenue field. Attribution is the input with the widest error bars, and it is also the only one you can fix in an afternoon rather than a quarter.","Run the calculation twice: once for the way the month actually went, and once with your hours reduced by the batching estimate. The gap between the two results is the value of changing how you work rather than how much you work.","Recalculate every quarter and keep the old results. A single ROI figure is an argument; four of them in a row is a trend, and a trend is what gets a channel defended in a budget meeting."],faqs:[{question:"How do you calculate social media ROI?",answer:"Profit divided by total cost, expressed as a percent. The part people skip is the cost side. Total cost here is the money you spend on tools plus the money your time is worth, which is weekly hours multiplied by 4.345 weeks and then by an hourly rate. Profit is the revenue you attribute to social minus that total. A 100 percent return means the channel gave back twice what it consumed; zero means it broke even; a negative number means it cost more than it returned, which is common early and is not automatically a reason to stop."},{question:"What hourly rate should I use?",answer:"The rate that reflects what the hour costs the business, not the one that flatters the result. For an employee, start with the annual salary divided by 2,080 working hours, then add roughly 25 to 30 percent for payroll taxes, benefits and overhead. For a freelancer or an agency, use the invoiced rate. For a founder doing the work personally, use the rate you would have to pay someone to replace you, which is usually far higher than the number a founder instinctively picks and is the whole point of pricing it at all."},{question:"What counts as attributed revenue?",answer:"Revenue you could defend in a meeting. Orders placed through a tracked link, sign ups that came in on the profile link, deals where the buyer named social media as the source, revenue from a campaign that ran nowhere else. What does not count is total business revenue for the month, revenue you assume social influenced, or anything justified by a rise in followers. Under-counting is the safer error: an ROI built on generous assumptions is a number nobody can act on, and one built on tracked revenue tells you exactly which activity to do more of."},{question:"Why 4.345 weeks in a month rather than four?",answer:"Because four undercounts the year by three whole weeks. A year is 52.18 weeks across 12 months, which is 4.345 weeks per month, so pricing labour at four weeks quietly understates it by about eight percent. On ten hours a week at fifty dollars an hour that gap is roughly 173 dollars a month, or two thousand a year, sitting outside the number you would take into a budget conversation. The constant is fixed at three decimals and used identically on this page and in the emailed breakdown so the two can never disagree."},{question:"Is a negative social media ROI normal?",answer:"Early on, yes, and it usually means one of two things rather than failure. Either the channel is genuinely young, in which case the compounding has not started and the honest read is that you are still paying for an audience you have not finished building, or the attribution is missing, in which case real revenue is being counted as zero because nothing traced it. Check the tracking first. It is far more often a measurement problem than a marketing one, and it is much cheaper to fix."},{question:"Do I get the full breakdown by email?",answer:"Yes. The return on your total monthly cost and the total itself are free on this page with no address at all, and the full worked example below runs the same arithmetic on real numbers so you can read the method without entering anything. The complete line by line breakdown, labour cost, tool cost, total, attributed revenue, profit, hours returned by batching and what those hours are worth, unlocks when you ask us to send it, and it is built from exactly the same formula and the same constants you can read on this page."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-audit","roas-calculator","ads-budget-calculator","engagement-rate-calculator","content-calendar-generator","cpa-calculator"]},"social-media-audit":{slug:"social-media-audit",family:"audit",platform:null,datePublished:g,lastUpdated:g,label:"Social media audit",metaTitle:"Social Media Audit (Free 12 Question Score)",metaDescription:"Free social media audit. Answer 12 questions on profile, cadence, engagement and ads readiness, get a score out of 100 and the four steps that follow.",heroEyebrow:"Free tool",heroTitle:"Social Media Audit",heroLead:"Twelve questions across profile, cadence, engagement and ads readiness, scored out of 100. Your total, your band and your weakest category are free on this page. The four step action plan written for the band you land in comes by email.",intro:["An audit is not a report card, it is a map of where to spend the next month. Most accounts that feel stuck are not weak everywhere: they are strong in two areas, adequate in one and quietly broken in a fourth, and the broken one is holding down everything the other three produce. A profile nobody understands wastes every visit a good post earns. A feed that goes quiet for three weeks undoes the audience a good profile collected. Finding the weak link is worth more than another posting tip, and it takes about three minutes of honest answers.","This audit covers the four areas that decide whether a social channel works. Profile is what a stranger sees in the two seconds after they tap your name: whether the bio explains who you help, where the link sends them, and whether the photo, the name and the first row of posts read as one thing. Cadence is the supply side: how often you publish, how far ahead it is planned, how many formats you rotate. Engagement is the part most accounts under-invest in: how fast you reply, whether you comment anywhere other than your own posts, and whether what people ask you decides what you post next. Ads readiness is the question of whether you could put money behind any of it tomorrow: tracking, a bank of proven creative and one clear offer.",'The scoring is deliberately plain. Twelve questions, three in each category, and every question has four concrete options rather than an agree-to-disagree scale, because "within 24 hours most of the time" is something you can answer and "somewhat agree" is not. The option you pick is the score it carries, from 0 for the weakest answer to 3 for the strongest, so a category is worth 9 points and the whole audit is worth 36. That raw total is rescaled to a percentage out of 100 for the headline, and every question counts the same: there is no hidden weighting pushing you towards a conclusion.',"Four bands sit on that 0 to 100 scale, and each one changes what the sensible next step is rather than just how the number feels. Below 41 the foundations are missing and two basic fixes beat any tactic. Between 41 and 60 the habit exists but the system does not. Between 61 and 80 most of it works and the remaining gains are in details you have been putting off. Above 80 the constraint stops being effort and becomes leverage. The plan you get is written for the band the whole audit landed in, which is on purpose: four disconnected verdicts is how an audit turns into a to-do list nobody starts, and one coherent set of next steps is how it turns into a month of work."],tips:["Answer as your account looked over the last month, not as you intend it to look. An audit scored on intentions returns a plan for an account you do not have yet, and the questions are written to be answerable from memory precisely so nobody is tempted to check first and round up.","Fix the weakest category before you improve the strongest one. Going from 8 to 9 in an area that already works changes almost nothing, and going from 2 to 5 in the one holding everything back changes the output of all four.","Read the profile questions with a stranger in mind. Everything in your bio is obvious to you and to the people who already follow you, which is exactly why those three questions are the ones most accounts overestimate themselves on.","Treat a low ads readiness score as a stop sign rather than a gap to fill with budget. Without tracking and one clear offer, paid traffic buys you a number you cannot interpret, and the cheapest month you will ever spend is the one before you start.","Score it again in 90 days and keep the first result. The absolute number matters far less than the direction, and the categories that refuse to move are telling you something that a single audit cannot.","Take the audit for one account at a time. Averaging three networks into one set of answers produces a score that describes none of them, and the weakest category is usually different on each."],faqs:[{question:"What is a social media audit?",answer:"A structured review of how an account is run, scored the same way every time so the result can be compared against itself later. It is not a look at your analytics and it is not a competitor comparison. This one asks twelve questions across profile, cadence, engagement and ads readiness, converts your answers into a score out of 100, and tells you which of the four areas is holding back the other three. The value is in the ranking rather than the number: knowing where you are weakest decides what the next month is for."},{question:"How is the score calculated?",answer:"Every question has four options, ordered from weakest to strongest, and the option you pick carries a score from 0 to 3. Three questions per category makes a category worth 9 points, and four categories make a raw total of 36. That raw total is rescaled to a percentage, so 18 out of 36 shows as 50 out of 100. Every question is weighted identically, which is a deliberate choice: any weighting scheme would be an opinion about your business dressed up as arithmetic, and an even one keeps the score readable as a map rather than a verdict."},{question:"What do the four bands mean?",answer:"Critical, 0 to 40, means the foundations are not in place and fixing two or three basics will move more than any posting trick. Developing, 41 to 60, means the habit exists but the system does not, and the next level comes from consistency and measurement rather than working harder. Solid, 61 to 80, means most of it is working and the remaining gains are in the details you have been putting off. Advanced, 81 to 100, means you are ahead of most accounts your size and your time is better spent on leverage than on maintenance."},{question:"What should I do with my weakest category?",answer:"Work on it first, and only it, for a month. The four areas are not independent: a strong feed pointed at a profile nobody understands loses the visit, and a perfect profile behind a feed that posted twice last quarter never gets the visit at all. Because the categories multiply rather than add, the return on lifting your worst score from 2 to 5 is larger than anything you can do to the score that is already at 8. The emailed plan gives you one concrete step per category, all four written for the band you landed in."},{question:"Do you look at my actual account?",answer:"No. There is no handle field, no follower count, no login and no connection to any network. The audit scores only what you tell it, which is why the questions are written to be answerable honestly in a few seconds from memory. That also means the result is exactly as useful as your answers are candid: scoring yourself generously produces a flattering number and a plan for somebody else. The questions, the options and the bands are all printed on this page, so you can read the entire rubric before answering anything."},{question:"Do I get the full action plan?",answer:"Yes, by email. Your total score, your band, its verdict and your weakest category are free on the page with no address at all, and the complete rubric with all twelve questions and every option is printed below either way. The four step action plan, one concrete next step for each category written for the band you scored in, plus your per-category breakdown out of 9, unlocks when you ask us to send it. It comes from exactly the same rubric you can read on this page."}],relatedToolSlugs:["social-media-roi-calculator","engagement-rate-calculator","best-time-to-post","content-calendar-generator","instagram-bio-generator","video-hook-generator"]}}),e.s(["TOOLS_DATA_VERSION",0,"2